Good — this is the right time to get organized. Below I’ve put together (A) focused questions to ask your attorney Maria Santos, (B) likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask at the preliminary hearing and later if the case goes to trial, (C) what to prepare and bring, and (D) practical preparation tips and next steps. You can bring this list to your meeting with Maria or I can draft an email to her with these items if you want.

A — Clear questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Procedural posture & goals
  - What is the specific relief we are seeking at the preliminary hearing (e.g., preliminary injunction, discovery stay, sanctions)?
  - What is the judge likely to decide at the preliminary hearing versus what requires trial?
  - What are the realistic case outcomes and timelines in the San Diego County civil division?

- Legal theory and burden
  - What elements of the nondisclosure agreement (NDA) the respondent must prove we can or cannot satisfy?
  - What is our burden to show breach, damages, or irreparable harm (for injunctive relief)?
  - Are there any affirmative defenses the respondent is likely to raise (waiver, ambiguity, consent, public policy, etc.)?

- Evidence & admissibility
  - Which documents/emails/messages are the most important and how should they be authenticated?
  - Are there hearsay or privilege issues we need to worry about (attorney-client communications, work product)?
  - Do we need any forensic or metadata analysis to authenticate electronic evidence?
  - Any risk of spoliation or need for a preservation order?

- Discovery & motions
  - What discovery do we need now (depositions, interrogatories, document requests)?
  - Should we move for a protective order, motion to compel, or a motion in limine before trial?
  - What discovery limitations or proportionality issues are likely?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who should we call as witnesses at the preliminary hearing and at trial?
  - Should we prepare expert witnesses (forensic, financial, damages, custody-related experts)?
  - Will you prepare me for direct and cross-examination (mock Q&A)?

- Custody / financial issues interplay
  - How might custody or financial facts affect the NDA dispute or the remedies available?
  - Could opposing counsel use custody/financial facts to attack credibility or motive — how should we respond?

- Strategy & settlement
  - What are the settlement options and what authority (if any) should I have to negotiate?
  - Should we attempt mediation before the hearing/trial?
  - What are the pros/cons of a public hearing vs. sealing records or redaction motions?

- Logistics and local practice
  - Which courtroom, judge, and local rules apply (San Diego County local rules)?
  - What is the expected hearing length, and what should I bring or wear?
  - Are there deadlines I must meet (exhibits, witness lists, exhibit numbering, prehearing statements)?

- Communication & conduct
  - How should I respond if opposing counsel contacts me directly?
  - What should I say or avoid saying publicly or on social media?

B — Likely questions opposing counsel may ask (preliminary hearing & trial)
- Questions on the NDA and facts
  - Did you sign the NDA? When and where?
  - What exactly did the NDA require you to keep confidential?
  - Did you or anyone acting for you disclose the protected information? When and how?
  - Who else had access to the information and why?
  - Were there any authorizations or exceptions to the NDA (e.g., disclosures to attorneys, required by law)?
  - What is the basis for your claim of damages or irreparable harm?
  - How did the respondent allegedly breach and what evidence ties them to the breach?

- Questions about credibility and motive
  - Why did you disclose the information (if any disclosure occurred)?
  - Do you (or a close family member) have a motive to exaggerate claims (financial, custody dispute, revenge)?
  - Have you told inconsistent versions of events to anyone (emails, messages, social media)?
  - Do you have any bias against the respondent?

- Questions that probe documents and timelines
  - Can you explain the timeline of communications (dates/times)?
  - How do you authenticate these emails/documents?
  - Did you delete any documents or devices? When?

- Points related to custody/financial matters
  - Are custody/financial disputes the real reason for this litigation?
  - How do the custody or financial facts influence the alleged harm from the NDA breach?

C — Likely questions or inquiries from the judge
- Case basics and procedural matters
  - What relief are you asking the court to grant today and on what legal basis?
  - What is the procedural posture (motions filed, discovery status, prior hearings)?
  - Is there a need for expedited relief? What is the urgency?
  - Have the parties attempted settlement or mediation?
  - Are there scheduling conflicts or reasons to continue or expedite the matter?

- If seeking injunctive relief (prelim. injunction)
  - What is the likelihood of success on the merits?
  - Will you suffer irreparable harm if relief is denied?
  - How would the balance of equities tip?
  - Is injunctive relief in the public interest?

- Discovery/dispute-related questions
  - What discovery remains and how long will it take?
  - Are discovery requests proportional and necessary?

D — What to prepare and bring
- Documents and evidence
  - Original NDA and any amendments
  - All emails, text messages, chat logs, attachments, and timeline of communications (with dates and participants)
  - Contract drafts or negotiations showing intent or scope
  - Financial records that relate to alleged damages or motives (bank statements, invoices)
  - Custody documents that could be relevant (court orders, parenting plans) — only if directly relevant per counsel
  - Any prior complaints, subpoenas, or related litigation documents

- Authentication & chain-of-custody
  - File metadata and device information for electronic evidence
  - Logs showing how/when documents were saved, shared, or modified
  - Witness contact information and summaries of their expected testimony

- Pre-hearing filings & evidence lists
  - Proposed exhibits, numbered and indexed
  - Witness list and estimated time for each
  - Pre-hearing brief or statement of facts (ask Maria to prepare)

E — Preparation & courtroom conduct tips
- Work with Maria to do mock Q&A: practice direct testimony and common cross-examination lines. Role-play is very effective.
- Keep answers short, truthful, and to the point. Don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you do not know or do not recall, say so — don’t guess.
- Avoid arguing with the judge or counsel in court; ask to confer with Maria if necessary.
- Preserve all evidence and stop posting about the case publicly; social media can be used against you.
- Ask Maria about appropriate courtroom attire and arrival time; know where to check-in and local security procedures.
- Learn how objections work so you understand when to pause and listen for attorney instruction.

F — Questions about logistics I can help with
- If you want, I can draft an email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting or pre-hearing checklist.
- I can also convert this into a one-page checklist you can bring to your hearing or create a mock Q&A script to rehearse answers.

Final note and disclaimer
- This is a planning and preparation checklist — it’s not legal advice. Maria Santos, as your attorney, should confirm legal strategy, relevance of custody/financial matters, and what to disclose at hearing or trial.

Would you like me to:
1) Draft an email to Maria with these questions and request a meeting, or
2) Create a printable one-page checklist or mock Q&A for practice?

