Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I’m not a lawyer, but I can help you organize focused questions and prepare for what the judge or opposing counsel may ask so your meeting with Maria Santos is efficient and productive. Below is a practical checklist and suggested questions, plus likely courtroom/counsel questions and what to bring/prepare.

Quick context recap I’ll assume going forward (tell me if anything differs): NDA dispute in San Diego County civil division; preliminary hearing soon, possible trial later; opposing party = respondent; financial and custody details may be relevant.

1) Short disclaimer to tell your attorney up front
- “I want to be clear I’m not asking for legal advice from anyone else — I want to prepare questions so our meeting is efficient.” (You don’t need me to repeat legal strategy; you only want to prep.)

2) Top questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case posture & timeline
  - What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing? What issues will the judge decide there?
  - What deadlines (discovery cutoff, motion cutoff, trial readiness dates) should I calendar now?
- Legal elements & standards
  - What specific elements must we prove for breach of the NDA (and for enforceability)?
  - What legal defenses should we anticipate from the respondent (e.g., ambiguity/overbreadth, unconscionability, prior disclosure, lack of consideration, public policy)?
  - What is the burden of proof at the preliminary hearing vs at trial?
- Evidence & proof
  - Which documents will be most important to prove our case (signed NDA, communications, disclosures, damages, timeline)?
  - Do we need witness testimony, expert(s), or forensic evidence (e.g., metadata, device logs)?
  - Are there specific authentication steps for electronic evidence I should take now?
- Discovery & urgent motions
  - What discovery should we seek immediately (emails, phone records, bank records, custody-related documents)?
  - Should we file any early motions (e.g., motion for preliminary injunction, protective order, expedited discovery, or to compel)?
- Custody/financial relevance
  - How could the custody or financial issues be used against me or in support of our case?
  - Is there a risk that family/custody matters will be used to prejudice the court on the NDA issue? How do we limit that?
- Settlement & alternatives
  - Is settlement advisable now? If so, what are our minimum/maximum settlement parameters?
  - What confidentiality protections and non-disparagement terms should we demand in a settlement?
- Practical & tactical
  - What testimony or documents would you like me to prepare/bring to the hearing?
  - How should I respond if opposing counsel asks about [specific sensitive topic]?
  - What should I say (and not say) in communications with the respondent or third parties going forward?
- Fees & cost exposure
  - What is the likely cost range through the preliminary hearing and, separately, through trial?
  - Are there contractual attorney-fee provisions in the NDA we can rely on (or that we need to defend against)?
- Logistics & courtroom prep
  - What will the hearing look like (who speaks first, time limits, judge’s preferences)?
  - Should we prepare a short factual timeline and exhibit notebook for the judge?
  - Can we do a mock Q&A before the hearing?

3) Likely questions from the judge (prepare short, factual answers)
- “What relief are you seeking?” — Be ready to state the remedy (damages, injunctive relief, attorneys’ fees).
- “What evidence supports a breach/enforceability of the NDA?” — Have 2–3 strongest exhibits/points ready (signed NDA, dated email showing disclosure, damages or risk of ongoing disclosure).
- “Have you tried to resolve this without court intervention?” — Be ready to summarize settlement efforts or communications.
- “Are there any scheduling or discovery disputes we should resolve now?” — Have specific requests/dates in mind.
- For preliminary injunction requests: “Why is irreparable harm likely and why are other remedies inadequate?” — Prepare factual examples of irreparable harm (loss of trade secrets, ongoing disclosures).
- If custody/financial issues are mentioned: “How is that relevant to this civil matter?” — Be ready to explain relevance succinctly (e.g., financial position is relevant to claimed damages or to credibility).

4) Likely questions from opposing counsel (and short prep points)
- “When did you first learn of the alleged disclosure/breach?” — Know the exact date(s) and which documents/emails support that date.
- “Who else had access to the information?” — Prepare a list of recipients, systems, and any access logs.
- “What damages did you suffer and how did you calculate them?” — Bring documentation for any financial loss, lost business opportunities, valuation methods.
- “Did you or anyone else waive or consent to disclosure?” — Be ready to address any written or verbal consents.
- Questions probing custody/finances: “Does custody/financial stress affect your credibility?” — Discuss with your attorney how to respond and what is relevant; don’t volunteer unnecessary details.
- “Do you have any communications showing they intended to disclose?” — Have the communications and their context available.

5) Documents and evidence checklist to bring to your attorney meeting and to the hearing
- Original or signed copy of the NDA (with dates and signatures)
- All related communications (emails, texts, DMs) in chronological order
- A short, dated timeline of events (who said/did what, when)
- Copies of any allegedly disclosed materials (what was disclosed; where published; screenshots)
- Access logs, metadata, device forensic reports if available
- Financial records showing damages, lost revenue, or costs
- Custody-related documents only insofar as they are relevant (court orders, support documents) — discuss with attorney whether to include
- Witness list with short summary of each witness’s expected testimony
- Prior settlement communications, demand letters, cease-and-desist letters
- Any contracts or prior NDAs that bear on interpretation
- Exhibits organized and Bates-stamped if possible; one exhibit notebook for counsel/judge

6) Custody / financial matters: special considerations
- Relevance: Ask Maria to explain how custody/financial information is relevant and whether a protective order is advised to prevent undue disclosure.
- Privacy: Consider filing a motion for in camera review or sealing sensitive documents if they must be filed.
- Evidence risk: Opposing counsel may try to use financial/custody issues to attack credibility — strategize with the lawyer on limiting scope and objecting where irrelevant.
- Expert testimony: If financial damages are complex, ask about a damages expert and how custody-related financial info might factor into valuations.

7) Pre-hearing prep steps to request from your attorney
- Mock Q&A and cross-examination practice with your attorney
- A short witness prep plan (what witnesses should say and what to avoid)
- A prioritized exhibit list and pre-marking exhibits
- Protective order or confidentiality stipulation draft (if sensitive material will be produced)
- Plan for any emergency relief (preliminary injunction or emergency discovery) with filing timeline

8) Courtroom & conduct tips to review with your attorney
- Keep answers short and factual; don’t speculate.
- If you don’t know an answer, say you don’t know — don’t guess.
- Ask your attorney before volunteering background facts that aren’t directly asked.
- Follow courtroom decorum (address judge appropriately; no interruptions).

9) Suggested meeting agenda with Maria (30–60 minutes)
- 5 min: Confirm objectives for hearing and desired outcomes
- 10 min: Walk through timeline & key facts/documents
- 10 min: Evidence & witness plan
- 10 min: Anticipate judge/opposing counsel questions and practice short answers
- 5–25 min: Tactical decisions — motions to file, settlement posture, next deadlines

10) Next steps I can help with
- Draft a one-page factual timeline you can bring to Maria (I can format it if you give me dates/short notes).
- Create a tailored list of anticipated questions and short model answers to rehearse.
- Generate a document checklist and exhibit index you can print.

If you want, supply the key dates/events (when NDA signed, when disclosure allegedly occurred, any communications/depositions already taken, preliminary hearing date) and I’ll draft the one-page timeline plus a targeted set of practice Q&A for the hearing and deposition.

