I can definitely help. Below I’ve put together: (A) likely questions the judge and opposing counsel may ask at the preliminary hearing or later at trial, (B) focused questions you should ask your attorney (Maria Santos) so you’re prepared, (C) a practical document/witness checklist to bring to your meeting, and (D) short tips for answering questions in court or at depositions. I also have a draft email ready to send to Maria if you’d like — tell me and I’ll prepare it.

Before I start, two quick clarifying questions:
- Are you the party who filed the case (petitioner/plaintiff) or are you the respondent/defendant?
- Are there minors involved (custody is active in this same case), or are the custody/financial matters part of a separate family/court file that might overlap?

A. Questions the judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask
(These are examples of what you might be asked directly or what opposing counsel will try to establish.)

About the NDA itself
- Who signed the agreement and on what date? Where was it signed?
- Was the NDA properly executed (signatures, dates, witness/notary if required)?
- What specific information is covered by the NDA (point to exact clauses)?
- What consideration was provided for the NDA (employment, payment, equity, settlement)?
- Was the NDA in effect at the time of the alleged disclosure?
- Were there any amendments, side agreements, or conflicting agreements?

About the alleged disclosure / breach
- What exactly was disclosed (specific documents, statements, dates)?
- When and how did the disclosure occur (email, phone, in-person, social media)?
- To whom was the information disclosed?
- Were any disclosures authorized or made with your consent?
- Was there any intent to disclose (accidental vs. willful)?
- What steps did you take to protect the information (passwords, labels, limited access)?

About damages and remedy
- What harm resulted from the disclosure (financial loss, reputational harm)?
- How are damages calculated or evidenced?
- Have you taken steps to mitigate the harm? If so, what?
- Do you seek injunctive relief (stop further disclosures), monetary damages, or both?
- Can the respondent pay any judgment (financial ability, insurance)?

About process, timing, and communications
- When did you first learn of the disclosure and when did you notify the respondent?
- Have settlement discussions occurred? Were there offers or demands?
- Have privileged communications been maintained? (opposing counsel will probe to see if privilege was waived)

About custody/financial overlap (if relevant)
- Are custody or financial situations relevant to motive or damages (e.g., disclosure to influence custody, to obtain financial advantage)?
- Are there existing court orders (custody, support) that could be affected by disclosures or filings?
- Has any party threatened to use confidential information in custody proceedings?

Credibility and background
- Were there prior incidents of similar conduct by either party?
- Are there any conflicts of interest, prior statements, or inconsistent testimony that the judge should know about?

B. Questions you should ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
Use these to guide your attorney meeting — they’ll help you confirm strategy, evidentiary issues, and how to prepare.

Case strategy and immediate goals
- What is our objective at the preliminary hearing? (e.g., get a protective order, preserve evidence, set trial date)
- What standards will the judge apply at this hearing?
- What are best/worst case scenarios at the hearing and at trial?

Evidence and admissibility
- Which documents and messages are strongest for proving breach? Which are weakest?
- Are any documents privileged or at risk of being excluded?
- What evidence do we need to authenticate (email headers, metadata, witnesses)?
- Can we seek a protective order or sealing of sensitive materials?

Witnesses and testimony
- Which witnesses are crucial and how should they be prepared?
- Will you recommend live testimony, affidavits, or both?
- Should we consider an expert (for damages, forensics, data recovery)?

Discovery and motions
- What discovery do we need immediately (emails, device forensics, financial records)?
- Should we file any pre-hearing motions (motion for protective order, motion to compel, motion in limine)?
- How long will discovery take and how aggressive should we be?

Settlement, mediation, and costs
- Should we propose mediation or settlement talks before the hearing?
- What settlement range is reasonable given the facts?
- What are expected costs if we proceed to trial (estimated attorney fees, expert fees)?

Custody/financial intersections
- How might custody or financial details be used against me?
- Do we need to bring family court documents or obtain separate protective measures?
- Will disclosures affect any child custody determinations? Can we ask for sealing of records to protect minors?

Courtroom behavior & testimony
- What should I say or avoid saying if asked directly by the judge?
- How should I answer questions from opposing counsel to avoid waiving privileges or giving unnecessary information?
- Should I testify at the preliminary hearing or wait until trial?

Jurisdiction and California-specific issues
- Are there California laws or local San Diego rules that affect NDA enforceability (e.g., limits on NDAs, public policy exceptions)?
- Are there local civil division procedures we need to follow for filings, exhibits, or emergency relief?

Logistics and timeline
- What’s the timeline to prepare before the preliminary hearing (documents, witness prep)?
- What should I do if new information appears between now and the hearing?
- Who will be lead at court — you or a colleague — and can I meet to rehearse?

C. Documents, evidence, and witness checklist to bring to your attorney meeting
Bring both originals (if available) and organized copies (electronic preferred and PDF):

Core documents
- The signed NDA and any related agreements or amendments
- Employment or contractual documents referenced by the NDA
- All communications referencing the confidential information (emails, texts, DMs, letters) in chronological order
- Any notices or warnings given under the NDA (e.g., confidentiality headers, labels)

Proof of disclosure / breach
- Copies/screenshots of the alleged disclosure(s) — dates, recipients, context
- Logs or metadata (email headers, IP logs) if you have them
- Social media posts or public disclosures showing the information was shared

Damages, financials, custody records
- Financial statements showing alleged losses or impact
- Invoices, bank records, or other economic evidence
- Any family/custody court orders or filings that may be relevant (if overlap exists)

Witnesses and corroboration
- Names and contact details of people who saw the disclosure, who can attest to the confidential nature of the info, or who observed harm
- Any written witness statements you already have

Procedural records
- Any prior correspondence about settlement or cease-and-desist letters
- Filings already submitted to the San Diego civil division
- Notices, court dates, or scheduling orders you received

Other
- Device chain-of-custody info if a phone/computer is relevant
- Expert reports (forensics, damages) if already obtained

D. Tips for answering questions at the hearing or in depositions
- Be concise and truthful. Short direct answers reduce openings for follow-up attacks.
- Do not guess — if you don’t know, say so and offer to find the answer.
- Avoid volunteering extra information beyond what’s asked.
- Refer technical/legal questions to your attorney when appropriate.
- Maintain calm composure, and speak slowly and audibly.
- If you need a break or to consult your attorney, ask for it politely.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise list of these questions formatted for you to print and bring to your meeting with Maria.
- Draft an email to Maria Santos requesting a preparation meeting and listing key points you want to cover.
- Role-play likely Q&A so you can practice short answers.

Tell me which of the above you want next (draft email, printable checklist, role-play practice), and confirm whether you’re the plaintiff or respondent and whether minors are involved.

