Key thing to confirm with your attorney before the hearing
- Is the upcoming appearance a preliminary hearing, case management conference, status conference, or another type of hearing in the civil division? (Different hearings have different focuses and required documents.)
- What is the immediate objective for that hearing (scheduling, temporary relief, discovery disputes, evidentiary rulings, settlement conference)?
- What is the likely timeline to trial under the court’s local rules and the assigned judge’s procedures?

Questions to ask Maria Santos about case strategy and process
- What is our theory of the case and the key legal elements we must prove for the NDA claim(s)?
- What are the respondent’s likely defenses (e.g., no valid NDA, authorized disclosure, information already public, lack of damages, waiver)?
- What standard of proof applies and what evidence will meet it?
- What motions should we expect (motions to dismiss, motions for summary judgment, motions in limine) and when will they be filed?
- Should we seek a protective order, sealing, or confidentiality designation for sensitive documents or hearings?
- What is our discovery plan and timeline (documents, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas)?
- What settlement/mediation posture do you recommend now? Is settlement likely or advisable before heavy discovery?
- What are the realistic outcomes and estimated costs to trial?

Questions about evidence and document preparation
- Which documents are most critical to prove the NDA: the signed agreement, communications about it, definitions of confidential information, access logs, witness statements?
- How should I preserve data and communications (phones, laptops, cloud accounts, social media, text messages, backups)? Should I take a litigation hold and stop deleting anything?
- Do we need forensic collection/experts (for emails, metadata, deleted files)? If so, when?
- What specific financial records will the respondent likely request or attempt to obtain, and how do we protect sensitive financial or custody-related records?
- Which documents should we redact or file under seal, and how do we do that under San Diego County procedures?
- Are there privileged communications we must preserve and a strategy for asserting privilege without waiving it?

Questions about custody and financial issues
- How could custody or financial matters be used against me (motive to disclose, mitigation of damages, relevance to remedy)?
- Will the respondent try to use custody/financial records to attack credibility or motive? How do we limit or object to that discovery or use at trial?
- Do we need a separate protective order or limitations on scope of discovery related to family/custody materials?
- If there are child-related or custody-sensitive documents, how are those best handled (redaction, sealed exhibits, in camera review)?

Questions about courtroom and procedural logistics
- Which judge will handle the case and what are that judge’s local preferences (exhibit formats, in-court technology, deadlines)?
- Will hearings (or depositions) be in person or remote? What do I need to bring/do for an in-person hearing?
- What exhibits do you want marked and ready for the preliminary hearing? Any demonstratives or timeline charts?
- What is the expected length of the hearing and who will speak for which issues?
- What is the procedure for swapping exhibits with opposing counsel and filing them with the court?

Questions to prepare about witness and testimony issues
- Who are our key witnesses and what will each testify to? Who might the respondent call?
- Should I prepare witness statements or affidavits in advance?
- How should I behave under questioning (short, truthful answers; don’t volunteer extra info; ask for clarification; take your time)?
- Do we want to do mock depositions or practice direct/cross with me?
- Are there vulnerable areas of my testimony (dates, recollection gaps) we should address now?

Specific questions judge might ask at the hearing (practice answers with Maria)
- What is the current status of discovery and are there disputes needing the court’s attention?
- What relief are you requesting from the court today and why is it necessary now?
- Can the parties agree on a proposed scheduling order/timeline?
- Is there pending or anticipated emergency relief (temporary injunction, TRO, seal)?
- Are there confidentiality issues that require in-camera review or a protective order?
- If custody/financial matters are relevant: why are they relevant to the claims and how should discovery be limited?

Specific questions opposing counsel is likely to ask (prepare factual, consistent answers)
- Was there a valid NDA in place? When was it executed and by whom?
- What exactly was claimed to be confidential, and how was that defined in the NDA?
- When and how did the alleged disclosure(s) occur? Who saw the information?
- Was the disclosure authorized or reasonably necessary for some business or family purpose?
- What damages or harm resulted from the disclosure and how were they calculated?
- Who had access to the confidential information (employees, family members, advisors)?
- Did you take steps to protect the information (passwords, restricted access, mark “confidential”)?
- Were there any prior breaches, or were portions already public or previously disclosed?
- For motive/credibility: do financial or custody disputes explain your conduct or motivations?
- Have you deleted or altered any documents or messages? (This triggers spoliation concerns — be careful: only answer with counsel’s guidance.)

Document and evidence checklist to bring to your meeting
- Original NDA(s) and any amendments
- All communications about the NDA (emails, texts, messaging apps) in chronological order
- A clean timeline of events with dates/times (when NDA signed, when disclosure allegedly occurred, when you learned of it)
- Any copies/prints/screenshots of disclosed material and locations where it was posted/shared
- Financial records that might be relevant (bank statements, invoices, payments tied to the disputed info)
- Custody orders, agreements, or filings that may affect relevance
- Logs or proof of access (server logs, document access, device logs)
- Witness contact info and short summaries of what each observed
- Any prior complaints, notices, or demand letters exchanged
- Records of mitigation steps you took (e.g., requests to takedown, cease-and-desist)

Practical tips for hearings, depositions, and meetings
- Be concise and truthful. If you don’t recall, say “I don’t remember” rather than guessing.
- Don’t volunteer extra background; respond to the question asked.
- If opposing counsel asks complex legal questions, defer to your attorney: “I’d prefer to have my attorney answer” (when appropriate).
- Practice a consistent narrative with Maria to avoid contradictions under cross-examination.
- Keep copies of all exhibits organized and indexed; produce what’s required but discuss protective measures first.
- Immediately preserve everything once litigation is anticipated: stop deleting relevant communications, take screenshots, back up devices.

Other strategic items to ask Maria
- Do we file a motion for temporary injunctive relief (if continuing disclosure is likely)?
- Should we seek sanctions or spoliation relief if we suspect evidence was destroyed?
- What is our public communications policy (social media posts, public statements)?
- How will custody/financial issues change our litigation costs or settlement leverage?
- What are the billing estimates and contingency options for moving to trial?

Offer to assist
- I can draft a one-page summary for Maria that lists our main factual timeline, exhibits, and the prioritized questions above.
- I can also create a short practice Q&A for the most likely judge/opposing counsel questions so you can rehearse answers.

Next step suggestion
- Print or email the checklist and timeline to Maria before your meeting so she can set priorities (and confirm the exact type of hearing and the judge). Ask her to mark which of the listed items are most urgent.

If you want, tell me:
- the rough timeline of key dates (when NDA signed, when the alleged disclosure occurred, hearing date), and
- which documents you already have collected.

I’ll draft a one-page summary and a short mock Q&A you can use in a meeting or a practice session with Maria.

