Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help you get organized. Below I’ve put together (A) focused questions to bring to your attorney (Maria Santos) so you cover legal strategy, evidence, and logistics; (B) likely questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask you at a preliminary hearing and later at trial (grouped by topic); and (C) practical preparation steps and documents to assemble. I can also create a mock Q&A script for practice if you want — tell me if you want that.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (prioritized)
- Case status & strategy
  - Exactly what stage is this case in (case management conference, hearing on temporary relief, preliminary injunction, trial scheduling)? What should I expect at the next hearing?
  - What is our overall theory of the case and the best outcome we should aim for (injunction, damages, dismissal, settlement)?
  - How likely are we to obtain temporary relief (e.g., a preliminary injunction) based on the facts we have?
  - What are the main legal weaknesses the respondent will attack and how will we address them?

- Specifics about the NDA and enforceability
  - Is the NDA enforceable under California law as to scope, duration, and subject matter? Are any clauses overbroad or likely to be invalid?
  - Who signed the NDA, and are there any signature/authority issues we should be concerned about?
  - Does the NDA define “confidential information” clearly enough? Will the respondent argue that information was public, independently developed, or not confidential?
  - Do trade secret protections (UTSA) apply? Could we pursue trade-secret remedies in addition to or instead of contract remedies?

- Relief & remedies
  - What relief will we seek at the next hearing (temporary restraining order, preliminary injunction, expedited discovery, fees)?
  - What burden must we meet to get a preliminary injunction in California (likelihood of success, irreparable harm, balance of equities, public interest)?
  - If we don’t get injunctive relief, what are the alternatives (monetary damages, accounting, disgorgement)?

- Evidence & discovery
  - What evidence will be decisive (emails, text messages, metadata, signed NDA, witness statements)? Which items should I collect and preserve now?
  - Do we need to issue preservation notices, subpoenas, or take immediate forensic steps (for deleted messages, backups, devices)?
  - Should we seek expedited or limited discovery before the hearing (e.g., production of allegedly disclosed documents, deposition of key witness)?
  - Are there privilege issues or risk of waiver if we disclose certain communications?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Will I be expected to testify at the hearing/trial? If so, what specifically will I be asked? Should we prepare a written declaration?
  - Which witnesses will be most helpful and which could be harmful? How should we prepare/warn them?

- Custody and financial matters (if relevant)
  - How might financial records or custody matters be used against me in this civil NDA dispute? Are they relevant to credibility or damages?
  - Could details from a custody case become part of the public record here? How can we limit disclosure or request sealing/in-camera review?
  - Do we need to obtain protective orders or redactions for sensitive financial/custody documents?

- Court logistics, timeline & costs
  - What are the upcoming deadlines (filing motions, discovery cutoff, hearings)? What’s our immediate checklist for the next 7–14 days?
  - What are likely costs for court filings, experts, discovery, and trial? Are contingency/alternative-fee options available?
  - Do we expect a jury trial? If so, do we want a jury? If not, why?

- Settlement & negotiation
  - What are realistic settlement options and ranges? When should we consider mediation?
  - Are there confidentiality safeguards for settlement talks (e.g., confidentiality clauses, sealing)?

B. Questions you should be ready to answer — what the judge or opposing counsel may ask
(Short answers and practice phrases — keep answers concise, factual, avoid speculation.)

- At a preliminary hearing or motion for injunctive relief (judge likely focuses on requirements for preliminary relief)
  - What specific provision(s) of the NDA do you claim the respondent breached?
  - What confidential information was disclosed and why is it confidential?
  - When and how did the disclosure occur (dates, channels — email, text, social media)?
  - What steps did you take to protect the information (passwords, limited access, markings, nondisclosure notices)?
  - What immediate harm did you suffer or will suffer if the respondent is not restrained?
  - Why is monetary compensation inadequate (i.e., why is injunctive relief necessary)?
  - What is the precise injunction you are asking the court to issue (narrowly tailored terms)?

- On admissibility and evidence (opposing counsel will probe authenticity and chain of custody)
  - Can you authenticate these emails/messages/documents? Who produced them and how were they stored?
  - Did you share the information with anyone else? If so, when and under what circumstances?
  - Have any documents been edited, deleted, or altered? Did you back up relevant devices?
  - Were any communications authorized or consented to by a third party?

- On intent and knowledge (opposing counsel will try to undermine your claims)
  - Did you ever give the respondent permission to use the information?
  - Were you aware of any public sources that contained the same information?
  - What was your relationship with the respondent (business, personal)? Could that affect credibility?

- On damages, finances, and custody (if these topics are relevant)
  - How have you quantified your damages? Provide specific financial losses, invoices, or lost contracts.
  - Are there financial motives that might affect your credibility (debts, bankruptcy, ongoing custody disputes)?
  - Is any part of your claim tied to a custody arrangement or support payments? If so, explain briefly — opposing counsel may seek to show bias or motive.
  - Have you discussed or shared details of custody matters with third parties whose testimony we now rely on?

- Cross-examination style questions (to prepare short, truthful answers)
  - “Isn’t it true you told X…?” (be prepared with timeline and precise wording)
  - “Why didn’t you act sooner?” (state reasons, show promptness when applicable)
  - “Didn’t you also share this with [other person]?” (answer honestly and explain context)

C. Practical preparation checklist
- Documents to collect and preserve right away
  - Original signed NDA and any related agreements or amendments.
  - All communications with respondent: emails, text messages, DMs, call logs, meeting notes, calendar invites.
  - Copies/screenshots of any disclosed information as it appeared publicly (dates/timestamps).
  - Logs showing who had access to the information.
  - Financial records showing alleged damages (invoices, lost-contract notices, bank statements).
  - Custody/financial court orders only if they are relevant — inform your attorney before producing them.
  - Device inventories (phones, laptops) and statements about deletion/backups.

- Evidence handling and forensics
  - Ask Maria about issuing a written preservation notice to the respondent and third parties.
  - Consider forensic imaging of devices if critical evidence may be deleted.
  - Maintain chain-of-custody notes for physical evidence.

- Witness prep
  - Identify and share with counsel all potential witnesses and what they can confirm.
  - Prepare short, factual declarations for hearing (signed, dated, notarized if advised).
  - Rehearse answers to likely cross-examination with counsel; keep answers concise and stick to facts.

- Protective measures
  - Discuss protective orders (confidentiality orders) and sealing of sensitive exhibits with your attorney.
  - Ask whether filings can be redacted or submitted under seal given custody/financial sensitivity.

- Courtroom conduct & testimony tips
  - Keep answers brief; do not volunteer extra information.
  - If you don’t remember, say so — avoid guessing.
  - Do not discuss the case with third parties; coordinate communications with your lawyer.

- Logistics to confirm with counsel
  - Who will speak at the hearing? Will you testify or is counsel presenting only?
  - Hearing length, required copies of exhibits, and local rules for San Diego County civil division (inquire about electronic filing and exhibit procedures).
  - Any travel/availability issues that could affect scheduling.

D. Questions for Maria specifically about local practice & procedure
- Have you handled similar NDA or preliminary injunction matters in San Diego County? Any judges or local rules I should know about?
- What is the expected timeline in our courthouse for motions, discovery, and trial settings?
- Can you give me a timeline and checklist for the next 2 weeks (what I need to deliver to you and when)?

E. Next steps I can do right now (if you want)
- I can draft a one-page timeline of events you can bring to Maria (dates, communications, witnesses).
- I can prepare a short mock Q&A for the most likely judge and opposing counsel questions so you can rehearse answers.
- I can draft the list of documents to assemble and a sample preservation notice template to discuss with Maria.

Quick legal reminder: I’m not your attorney — this is organizational and preparatory. Use these questions with Maria Santos to get legal advice tailored to your case and jurisdiction.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a one-page chronology for you to share with Maria?
- Create a mock Q&A script to practice (I can simulate judge and opposing counsel)?
- Or tailor the lists above if you tell me (a) the exact language of the NDA or (b) what was disclosed and how?

