Good — I can help you prepare. Below is a focused, organized set of things to ask your attorney (Maria Santos) and the likely questions a judge or opposing counsel may ask you. I also include practical prep tips, a documents checklist, and offers to draft a one-page question list or run a mock Q&A so you can practice.

Important: treat everything you discuss with your attorney as confidential. Use these prompts to guide your conversation with Maria — she’ll give legal advice tailored to your case.

1) High-level questions to ask your attorney now
- Case status & schedule
  - What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing in this civil/division matter, and what will the judge decide there?
  - What deadlines (discovery, motions, mediation, trial date) should I expect after the hearing?
  - How urgent is anything I need to produce or do before the hearing?

- Legal theory, burden, and remedies
  - What specific legal claims are we asserting (e.g., breach of contract, breach of NDA, misappropriation of trade secrets)?
  - What is the standard of proof for those claims, and who bears the burden of proof on each element?
  - What remedies are we seeking (damages, injunction, attorneys’ fees, return/destruction of materials)?

- Evidence & discovery
  - What documents, communications, and metadata are most important for proving breach and damages?
  - Have we served/received all necessary discovery? Are there outstanding discovery disputes we should bring up at the hearing?
  - Should we seek a protective order, sealing, or redaction for sensitive financial or custody-related materials?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses will we call (including expert witnesses)? Which witnesses might the respondent call?
  - Do we need declarations, affidavits, or witness statements filed for the hearing?
  - Should any witnesses be prepped for cross-examination now?

- Strategy & settlement
  - What is our best-case and worst-case scenario for the preliminary hearing and for trial?
  - Are there settlement or mediation options to pursue before the hearing or trial? What are settlement ranges?
  - Would pursuing a preliminary injunction or TRO be advisable? What are the pros/cons?

- Custody & financial issues
  - How could the custody or financial details affect the NDA claim or remedies here?
  - Do we need to keep family/custody records separate or sealed to protect privacy?
  - Might disclosure of financial/custody info create additional risks (e.g., in related family law matters)?

- Procedural & courtroom logistics
  - What should I expect to say or present at the preliminary hearing? Should I attend in person?
  - How should I dress and behave in court? Are there local rules for San Diego County civil division I should know?
  - Who will speak for us at the hearing — you or me? If I’m testifying, when and what is expected?

2) Questions the judge is likely to ask (preliminary hearing focus)
- What is the nature of the dispute and what relief are you asking the court to grant at this stage?
- What is your legal basis for claiming a breach of the NDA?
- Can you briefly summarize the timeline: when was the NDA signed, what was disclosed, when did the alleged breach occur, and when did you learn about it?
- What specific evidence supports your allegation of a breach (documents, emails, witnesses)?
- Are there any immediate harms or irreparable injury that justify interim relief (TRO or preliminary injunction)?
- Have you complied with discovery and court rules? Are there outstanding discovery issues?
- Is there a possibility of resolving this without full trial (mediation/settlement)?
- Are there any related cases (family law, criminal) or counterclaims the court should be aware of?

3) Questions opposing counsel (the respondent) is likely to ask you
- Can you confirm the dates and details of the NDA (signing date, parties, scope of confidentiality)?
- Exactly what information do you claim was confidential and how was it protected?
- Who had access to the information? Were there any prior disclosures or sharing?
- Why would the respondent have a motive to disclose (or not disclose) the information?
- How do you know the respondent disclosed it — what is the source of your evidence?
- What damages or harm resulted from the disclosure, and how are you calculating them?
- Are you seeking monetary damages only, or injunctive relief (stop further disclosure, return/destroy info)?
- Have you made any attempts to mitigate the harm (e.g., cease-and-desist, internal remediation)?
- For custody/financial issues: How is this dispute relevant to custody or support matters? Are you asserting misuse of financial/custody records?

4) How to prepare your answers — practical tips
- Keep answers concise, factual, and chronological. Practice a short, clear timeline you can state in 2–3 sentences.
- Do not volunteer extra details. Answer the question asked; if you don’t recall, say so and offer to check documents.
- Avoid sounding speculative—if you don’t know how something happened, say you’re not sure and point to the evidence that supports your position.
- Rehearse likely tough cross-examination lines: inconsistencies, prior communications, motives, and what you told others about the matter.
- Review all communications (emails, texts) and note dates, senders/recipients, and attachments. Be ready to identify originals and how they were stored.
- Work with Maria to draft declarations or a short witness statement for the hearing explaining key facts.

5) Documents and evidence checklist to bring (or prepare/send to counsel)
- The signed NDA (all versions) and any amendments.
- All relevant communications (emails, text messages, WhatsApp, social media DMs) showing relevant dates and parties.
- A documented timeline of events (one-page chronology).
- Evidence of disclosure (copies/screenshots, printouts, saved files, witness statements).
- Logs or metadata showing access or distribution (file access logs, email headers).
- Financial records that connect to the dispute (redact unnecessary personal data if needed; coordinate with counsel).
- Custody-related documents only if directly relevant — discuss sealing/redaction with Maria before filing.
- Any cease-and-desist letters, demand letters, or prior legal correspondence.
- Declarations from witnesses or experts (if applicable).
- Pleadings, discovery responses, proof of service, and court scheduling orders.

6) Questions about discovery, protective orders, and privacy
- Should we file a motion for a protective order before producing sensitive financial/custody docs?
- Can we request that certain filings be sealed or redacted in San Diego County? What’s required?
- How should I store/label documents to maintain chain-of-custody and privilege where applicable?
- Are there privilege issues (attorney-client communications, work product) we should assert?

7) Deposition and cross-examination preparation
- Ask your attorney to run a mock deposition and short cross-examination to practice calm, concise answers.
- Learn to handle leading questions and how to assert objections (your attorney will do objections; you should listen).
- Identify three “must-make” points you want to leave the judge/jury with; practice saying them clearly.
- Practice admitting small factual points promptly to keep credibility, but avoid volunteering legal conclusions or speculation.

8) Practical courtroom/logistics questions to ask Maria
- Will I be required to testify at the preliminary hearing or just at trial?
- Do we need any witnesses present at the preliminary hearing?
- What exhibits should be pre-marked or filed in advance with the court clerk?
- How long will my appearance likely take? Are remote appearances allowed?
- How should I communicate with you the night before / day of the hearing (phone, email)?

9) Questions about settlement and costs
- What are realistic settlement ranges given the facts so far?
- What are the anticipated legal costs to get through discovery, motions, and trial?
- If settlement is discussed at the hearing, what parameters should I authorize you to accept or reject?

10) Next steps I can take now (action items)
- Create a one-page timeline of events with dates, names, and key documents.
- Gather and label the documents in the checklist; send copies to Maria with notes about relevance.
- Schedule a prep session (mock Q&A) with Maria before the hearing.
- Decide whether to ask Maria to file a protective order or sealing motion for sensitive records.
- Prepare a short list of questions for the hearing (if you’ll speak) and run those by Maria.

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn the above into a concise one-page list you can email to Maria.
- Create a mock Q&A script (judge + opposing counsel questions with suggested concise answers) so you can practice.
- Draft a short email to Maria asking for a prep session and attaching the documents checklist.

Which of those would you like me to prepare next?

