3. Any instance(s) of the alleged disclosure: screenshots, copies of published material, emails showing distribution, witness statements.
4. Access logs, version history, server logs, or metadata proving access or copying.
5. Financial records showing damages or relevant financial context (invoices, contracts, bank statements).
6. Custody-related documents only if relevant and admissible — parenting plans, court orders, or communications showing how custody/finances tie to the case.
7. Evidence of confidentiality practices: policies, NDAs with others, training, IP registrations.
8. Prior disputes, threats, settlement offers, or communications that bear on motive.
9. Chain of custody and preservation evidence (to show evidence hasn’t been altered).
10. Any public statements or social media posts by you or the respondent that touch the facts.

