Good—this is a smart way to prepare. Below I’ve organized practical, attorney-focused questions you can ask Maria Santos plus likely questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask at the preliminary hearing (and later at trial), what documents to prepare, and short tips for answering. Use this as a checklist when you meet or call your lawyer.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & procedure
  - What exactly should I expect at the upcoming preliminary hearing in the civil division (purpose, format, likely length)?
  - What are the next procedural milestones if the case proceeds to trial (discovery deadlines, motion cutoffs, pretrial conference, trial date ranges)?
  - Are there any San Diego County local rules or judge-specific practices I should know about for hearings and exhibit filing?
- Strategy & likely legal arguments
  - What is our legal theory and the respondent’s likely defenses in this NDA dispute?
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of our case and of the respondent’s case?
  - Do you expect the respondent to move to dismiss, file a special motion (e.g., anti-SLAPP), or seek summary judgment? How would we respond?
- Evidence & discovery
  - What documents and communications do we need to collect and produce (emails, texts, drafts, payment records, meeting notes)?
  - What discovery do we need from the respondent (specific requests for production, interrogatories, depositions)?
  - Should we seek a protective order, sealing, or in-camera review for any sensitive financial/custody information?
- Damages & remedies
  - What damages or remedies are we seeking (injunctive relief, monetary damages, declaratory relief, attorney’s fees)?
  - How will the respondent’s financial or custody circumstances affect damages or equitable relief arguments?
- Witnesses & preparation
  - Which witnesses will we call and what will they testify to? Do we need witness statements prepared in advance?
  - Should we prepare you for deposition/testimony now? Can we do a mock Q&A?
- Practical & settlement
  - What settlement options do you recommend and when should we consider settlement discussions?
  - If the respondent makes an offer at a hearing, may I accept or should I always defer to you?
- Costs & logistics
  - What are estimated costs to get through the hearing, discovery, and trial stages?
  - What do you need from me and by when (documents, a written chronology, contacts for witnesses)?
- Confidentiality / Family matters
  - How can we protect custody and financial information from becoming public record? Do we need sealing motions or redactions?
  - Could anything in this case affect custody proceedings or family court? Should we coordinate with family-law counsel?
- Communications & next steps
  - How will you communicate updates and when should I contact you with new information?
  - Can you walk me through how to respond if the judge or opposing counsel asks me a question directly at the hearing?

B. Questions the judge is likely to ask (preliminary hearing / case management context)
- Jurisdiction & standing: Are the parties properly before the court? Is venue proper in San Diego County?
- Case posture: What relief is the plaintiff seeking? Has the respondent been properly served?
- Factual overview: Briefly summarize the dispute and timeline—what is the NDA, when was it signed, what was allegedly disclosed/breached?
- Legal basis: What specific parts of the NDA are claimed to be breached? Are there contractual ambiguities?
- Evidence/readiness: Are parties ready for discovery and trial? Do you need any short continuances?
- Confidentiality/public access: Do you request sealing or protective orders for sensitive documents (financial or custody records)?
- Settlement efforts: Have the parties attempted to mediate or discuss settlement?
- Scheduling: Proposed deadlines for discovery, motions, and trial date windows.

C. Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask (or try to elicit)
- Factual details: Dates, exact content of alleged disclosures, who had access to the confidential material, who else knew about it.
- Intent/state of mind: Did you intend for the information to be kept confidential? Did you act to protect it (labels, emails saying “confidential”)?
- Contract terms: Interpretation of NDA provisions (definitions, duration, scope, exceptions).
- Damages and mitigation: What harm occurred? Did you mitigate damages? Are any losses speculative?
- Alternate explanations: Is there an innocent explanation for any disclosures or similarity in information?
- Credibility & personal information: Questions about finances, custody arrangements or personal conduct (they may probe if relevant to credibility or damages).
- Discovery issues: Requests for documents, contemporaneous communications, and witnesses.

D. Custody & financial details — what to be careful about
- Relevance: Ask Maria which family/financial details are actually relevant and which are not — opposing counsel may try to expand relevance.
- Privacy protection: Ask about protective orders, sealing, or limiting scope of produced material (redact unrelated sensitive info).
- Impact on remedies: Understand how respondent’s financial ability might affect monetary damages, and whether custody disputes could be used as leverage or to undermine credibility.
- Coordination with family law counsel: If there’s an active custody case, ask about coordinating discovery and preserving rights across courts.

E. Documents and materials to prepare now
- A chronological timeline of events related to the NDA (dates, people, communications).
- Original NDA and all revisions, emails, drafts, and signatures.
- All communications referencing the confidential information (emails, texts, messaging apps).
- Evidence of harm or misuse (screenshots, copies of disclosed materials, witness statements).
- Financial records that show damages or mitigation (invoices, bank transfers, lost business evidence).
- Any third-party communications or social media posts that may be relevant.
- List of potential witnesses with short notes on what each can attest to.
- Copies of any previous settlement offers, mediation notes, or relevant court filings.

F. Practice questions to rehearse (short answers)
- “When did you sign the NDA?” — state the date and context.
- “What exactly was disclosed and to whom?” — be specific and concise.
- “How did you attempt to keep the information confidential?” — give clear examples (marked documents, restricted access).
- “What harm did you suffer and how did you calculate damages?” — be factual and cite documents.
- “Have you shared this information with anyone else?” — answer truthfully; don’t volunteer extra detail.
- “Why is this information relevant to the case?” — tie to NDA terms and damages.

G. Tips for answering questions in court or deposition
- Be truthful and concise. Don’t guess—if you don’t remember, say so.
- Answer only the question asked; don’t volunteer unrelated details.
- Pause and think before answering. It’s ok to ask for clarification.
- If you don’t understand a legal term or question, ask for it to be rephrased.
- Follow your attorney’s instructions: if your lawyer objects, wait for guidance.
- Maintain calm, polite demeanor; avoid arguing with opposing counsel or the judge.
- If asked about sensitive family/financial matters, refer to your counsel if privileged or objecting is appropriate.

H. Procedural issues to confirm with Maria Santos
- Who will speak for you at the hearing and what will they present?
- Will we file any short pre-hearing briefs, exhibits, or witness lists ahead of time?
- How will exhibits be marked and exchanged for the hearing/trial (electronic or paper)?
- What’s the courtroom location and courtroom etiquette for the assigned judge?
- If the respondent requests a continuance or unexpected motion, what are our options?

I. Next steps I can help with
- I can draft a concise list/email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and request a prep meeting (if you want, tell me what tone and any specific points to include).
- I can help you build a chronology or an exhibits checklist from details you provide.
- I can create a short mock Q&A for practicing answers.

Would you like me to: (a) draft an email to Maria Santos with these questions, or (b) help you build a timeline and exhibits checklist now? If (a), tell me any additional specifics you want in the email (your availability, whether you want a phone call or in-person meeting, prefer a hearing prep session).

