I can help you get organized. Below I’ve put together (A) a short checklist of clarifying facts I need from you, (B) focused questions you should ask your attorney (Maria Santos) grouped by topic, (C) likely areas/questions the judge or opposing counsel may raise and how to prepare, and (D) practical preparation tips and a documents checklist. If you want, I can draft an email to Maria with these questions so you can send it quickly.

A — Quick clarifying points (so I tailor this list)
- What exactly is the next hearing called and what is it for (preliminary injunction hearing, case management conference, hearing on a motion, preliminary hearing)? If you’re not sure, tell me the date and any notice you received and I can help parse it.
- Are there any existing protective orders, sealing requests, or confidentiality designations in place?
- Do you expect to testify at the hearing/trial?
- Do you have a deadline for discovery or depositions coming up?
If you want, answer those and I’ll refine the list.

B — Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
Case strategy & status
- What is our legal theory for why the NDA is enforceable (or unenforceable) and what elements must we prove at this stage?
- What are the respondent’s main defenses likely to be (e.g., lack of consideration, overbreadth, ambiguity, public policy, fraud, duress, waiver)?
- What is the immediate goal for the upcoming hearing and what are the realistic outcomes (e.g., dismissal, injunction, scheduling, discovery orders)?
- What is our litigation timeline (motions, discovery cutoff, settlement conference, trial date estimate)?

Evidence & documents
- What documents are most important to prove breach/defense (signed NDA, emails/texts, drafts, witness emails, records of disclosure, financial records tied to alleged harm)?
- Do we have complete versions of all relevant communications (including metadata if relevant)? If not, how do we obtain them?
- What do we need to preserve now to avoid spoliation claims? (devices, backups, cloud accounts, Slack, social media)
- Are there documents we should file under seal or move to protect from public record?

Witnesses & testimony
- Who will we call as witnesses and what will each witness say?
- Will I be expected to testify at the hearing or trial? If so, what topics will I be asked about?
- Do any witnesses need preparation or testimony rehearsals/depositions?
- Are there potential hostile witnesses and how do we handle them?

Discovery & motions
- What discovery is outstanding and what is the plan to obtain it (subpoenas, third-party discovery)?
- Are we planning any dispositive or evidentiary motions (motion to dismiss, summary judgment, motion in limine)?
- Do we need a protective order for sensitive materials? If so, can you prepare and file it before discovery responses are produced?

Damages & remedies
- What remedies are we seeking or defending against (monetary damages, injunctive relief, attorneys’ fees)?
- How will damages be proved? What financial records or expert testimony will be needed?
- If injunctive relief is sought, what facts will the court consider (irreparable harm, balance of hardships, public interest)?

Custody & financial details (sensitivity & relevance)
- How relevant are the custody/financial details to the NDA dispute? If they are only tangential, can we avoid bringing them into evidence?
- Can we get records sealed or excluded if they’re private and not relevant?
- What safeguards can we put in place to prevent disclosure of sensitive family/custody information (protective order, redaction, in camera review)?
- Could custody/financial matters be used against me as credibility or motive evidence and how do we mitigate that risk?

Interactions with opposing counsel / settlement
- What is our settlement posture and negotiation limits (floor/ceiling, non-monetary terms you’d accept)?
- Should we consider mediation or settlement before the hearing/trial?
- What are the risks of informal communications with the respondent or their counsel? (Confirm whether your attorney wants all communications routed through them.)

Procedural & logistics for the hearing/trial
- What will happen at the upcoming hearing (agenda, who speaks first, expected length)?
- Do I need to file anything in advance (declarations, exhibit lists, witness lists)?
- Are there local rules in San Diego County Civil Division I should be aware of (local forms, exhibit procedures, courtroom technology)?
- Will the hearing be in person, by phone, or by video? If in person, what time should I arrive and what ID or check-in process is required?

Privilege & communications
- Which communications are protected by attorney-client or work-product privilege and how do we assert it if necessary?
- If there are third-party communications (e.g., with consultants), how do we minimize disclosure risk?

Costs, timeline & expectations
- What are the likely costs through the next critical milestone (hearing, discovery phase, trial)?
- What are our best- and worst-case scenarios moving forward?
- What decisions will I need to make soon (e.g., settlement, consenting to certain disclosures)?

C — Questions you might be asked by the judge or opposing counsel (and how to prepare)
Judge — likely procedural and focused questions
- “What relief are you asking the court to grant?” — Be ready to state specific remedies requested.
- “What is the basis for federal/state/county jurisdiction?” — Make sure counsel has the jurisdictional facts prepared.
- “What is the schedule you are proposing?” — Know the discovery deadlines you and counsel want.
- For preliminary injunctions: “Have you shown likelihood of success on the merits? Irreparable harm? Balance of harms? Public interest?” — Ask Maria how our facts map to each element.

Opposing counsel (direct/cross-examination themes)
- Timeline and details of communications: “When did you sign the NDA? Who else saw these communications? When did you first become aware of the alleged disclosure?” — Have a clear, documented timeline.
- Intent and knowledge: “Did you know the information was confidential? Did you ever disclose it yourself?” — Be factual and concise; don’t volunteer extra info.
- Motive: “Did you have a reason to harm the other party or gain financially?” — Prepare truthful, non-argumentative responses; counsel will prep you.
- Custody/financial probing (if relevant): Counsel may try to use personal or financial facts to challenge credibility or motive. Ask Maria what lines are off-limits and when to object.

How to respond in court/at deposition
- Be truthful, concise, and stick to the question asked.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so — don’t guess.
- If a question calls for privileged communications, tell counsel you will confer with your attorney.
- If a question is confusing, ask for clarification before answering.

D — Practical preparation + documents checklist
Preparation steps
- Schedule a mock Q&A / deposition prep with Maria (ask for one if not scheduled).
- Create a clear chronological timeline of events and circulate to your attorney for review.
- Flag any sensitive family or financial records and discuss sealing/redaction options with Maria.
- Discuss courtroom demeanor: short answers, pause to think, will you be allowed to confer with counsel.

Documents to gather and organize
- Signed NDA and all draft versions
- All communications related to the subject matter (emails, texts, Slack, DMs, social posts)
- Evidence of alleged disclosures (published documents, emails to third parties)
- Financial records tied to damages (invoices, bank statements, profit/loss evidence)
- Custody-related records only if directly relevant (court orders, custody schedules) — discuss before producing
- Witness contact info and any prior statements/declarations
- Any prior settlement offers/communications (be careful about what is admissible)

Final notes / suggested questions to actually ask Maria in your next meeting (short list)
- “What is the exact purpose of the upcoming hearing and what should I expect to happen?”
- “Which documents do you want me to bring and in what order?”
- “Do you think I’ll need to testify? If so, can we do a practice session?”
- “How can we protect custody/financial records from public disclosure?”
- “What are our settlement parameters and timeline?”

