I can help — I’ll outline (A) focused questions you should ask your attorney, (B) likely questions a judge may ask at a preliminary/case-management hearing, (C) likely lines of questioning opposing counsel may use (direct and cross-examination style), (D) documents and evidence to get organized, and (E) practical tips for responding in court or depositions. I’m not a lawyer — this is preparation guidance only. Ask Maria Santos to confirm any legal strategy or filings.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status and timetable
  - What type of hearing is this preliminary hearing (case management conference, temporary orders, motion hearing)? What is the judge likely to decide at this hearing?
  - What is the timeline from this hearing to potential trial? Key upcoming deadlines (discovery, motions, settlement conference, pretrial conference)?
- Legal theory and burden
  - What specific claims are we asserting (breach of contract, injunction, damages)? What elements must we prove for each claim?
  - What is the respondent’s likely legal defense to each claim?
- Evidence and proof
  - Which documents and communications are most important to prove breach or rebut their defenses?
  - Do we have admissible evidence of intent, breach, and damages (emails, text messages, signed NDA, witness testimony, financial records)?
  - Are any portions of the evidence privileged or sensitive? How should privileged material be handled?
- Discovery and evidentiary process
  - What discovery should we seek (emails, bank records, device data, custody-related documents)? What do we expect to receive from the respondent?
  - Are there pending or planned motions (protective order, motion to compel, motion for temporary restraining order, anti-SLAPP, summary judgment)?
  - Should we seek a protective order or request to seal records because of confidentiality/family/custody matters?
- Witnesses and testimony
  - Who will testify for us? Should I testify at the preliminary hearing or only at trial/deposition?
  - How will we prepare witnesses? What are the key points each witness should be ready to state?
- Remedies, damages, and financial/custody implications
  - What remedies are we seeking (injunction, monetary damages, attorneys’ fees)? How will custody/financial facts influence damages or ability to pay?
  - Will disclosures about custody or finances be required? How do we minimize exposure of sensitive family information?
- Settlement and negotiation
  - What settlement options are reasonable now? Are there confidentiality/ND terms we want as part of any settlement?
  - Would settlement negotiations be confidential or protected?
- Courtroom logistics and conduct
  - What should I say or avoid saying at the hearing? How should I handle questions from the judge or opposing counsel?
  - Any procedural preferences for San Diego Civil Court (local rules, filing requirements, evidence format)?
- Costs and strategy choices
  - What is the likely cost of proceeding to trial versus settling? What are the realistic chances at trial?
  - Are there tactical motions we should consider before the preliminary hearing?
- Next steps
  - What do you need from me in the next 7–14 days (documents, witness contact info, timeline of events)?

B. Questions a judge may ask at a preliminary/case-management hearing
- Basic case management / status questions
  - What are the principal issues that remain to be litigated?
  - What is each party’s position on the case schedule (discovery cutoff, dispositive motion deadline, trial date)?
  - Are there any settlement efforts or mediation planned?
- On evidence and scope
  - What evidence do you expect to rely on to prove the elements of your claim?
  - Are there confidentiality concerns that require sealing or a protective order?
- On interim relief
  - Are you requesting any temporary relief (injunction, discovery stay, protective order) at this stage?
- On discovery or disputes
  - Are there outstanding discovery disputes? Do either side seek permission for expedited discovery?
- On witnesses and testimony
  - Will any party need to present witnesses at this hearing? Will the parties be testifying about custody/financial issues?
- On case narrowing
  - Are there issues that can be narrowed by stipulation or summary adjudication?
- Logistics
  - Do you anticipate anything that will significantly delay the case?

C. Likely lines of questioning from opposing counsel (and things to prepare)
Note: opposing counsel will try to challenge your credibility, minimize damages, or show compliance with the NDA. Prepare concise factual responses and corroborating documents.

Direct (what they will ask their own witnesses)
- Can you describe the events and communications between you and the respondent relevant to the NDA?
- How and when did the alleged disclosure/breach occur?
- Did you take any steps to prevent disclosure? When did you discover the breach?
- What harm did you suffer (financial, reputational)? How did you calculate damages?
- Who else had access to the information and why?

Cross-examination (how they may question you)
- Were you aware of all the terms of the NDA? Is this your signature on the agreement?
- Did you ever waive confidentiality or disclose information to third parties?
- Have you used or benefited from the disputed information since the alleged breach?
- Why did you not take earlier steps to mitigate damages?
- Are there inconsistencies between your statements and your documents/emails?
- How are custody/financial matters relevant — are you trying to leverage these issues in this dispute?
- Have you been involved in similar disputes or made different statements earlier?

Lines questioning custody/financial issues (if opposing counsel raises them)
- What is your current financial position and how does it relate to claimed damages?
- Are custody arrangements or parenting time schedules relevant to communications at issue?
- Have any custodial disputes involved communications referenced in this case?

D. Documents and evidence checklist to organize (bring these to your meeting with Maria)
- The signed NDA(s) — all versions, attachments, amendments.
- Full timeline of events (dates/times) with short descriptions of each event.
- All communications (emails, texts, direct messages, social media messages) between you and the respondent and any third parties referencing the confidential matter.
- Drafts, edits, or file metadata showing creation/transfer of the confidential material.
- Proof of dissemination (copies of where information appeared if published/shared).
- Financial records showing claimed damages (invoices, loss statements, bank/transaction records).
- Records relevant to custody (custody orders, parenting plans) only if they are potentially relevant; flag any sensitive family info for counsel.
- Witness list with contact info and short summary of what each witness can confirm.
- Any prior settlement offers, demand letters, or cease-and-desist communications.
- Discovery documents already exchanged and any responses/reservation-of-rights.
- Any public postings, press, or third-party use of the confidential information.
- Privilege log or notes about documents that might be privileged.

E. Practical courtroom and deposition tips
- Be concise and factual. Answer only the question asked; avoid volunteering extra details.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.
- Ask for clarification if a question is confusing. Take pauses before answering.
- Avoid argumentative or emotional responses in front of the judge. Keep tone calm.
- If asked about family/custody matters, ask your attorney whether to answer or to request that questioning be limited/obtained via sealed filings or protective order.
- Have documents organized and tabbed so you can quickly produce an exhibit if asked.
- Practice key testimony points with your attorney; rehearse responses to likely cross-examination lines.
- Ask the judge for a brief recess if you need to consult your attorney during the hearing.

F. Concrete next actions I can help with
- Draft a concise list of questions to send to Maria Santos (I can draft an email).
- Build a chronological timeline template you can fill in with dates and key facts.
- Create a witness-exhibit index you can bring to meetings.
- Role-play judge or opposing counsel questions so you can practice answers.

