- The NDA itself (all versions, fully executed copies)
- All communications referring to the NDA subject matter (emails, texts, chat logs, recorded calls)
- Drafts and internal notes about the confidential matter
- Evidence of distribution or publication (where/how information was disclosed)
- Financial records if damages involve monetization (bank statements, invoices, contracts)
- Custody documents only if directly relevant; otherwise, documentation showing why custody info is irrelevant
- Witness contact info and written statements (colleagues, recipients of alleged disclosure)
- Metadata or forensic copies if digital evidence is critical (don’t alter originals)
- Any prior agreements, employment contracts, or policies that define confidentiality

5) How custody/financial issues can affect the case — and how to protect them
- How they may be used:
  - As evidence of motive (need for money, retaliation)
  - To attack credibility (inconsistent statements, character evidence)
  - To affect damages (ability to pay or extent of harm)
- Protection steps:
  - Ask your attorney about motions in limine, protective orders, or sealed filings.
  - Limit admission of collateral personal issues unless they are directly probative.
  - Keep sensitive documents off public filings until the court orders otherwise.

6) Preparing your testimony — practical tips
- Keep answers short, truthful, and only responsive to the question asked. Avoid volunteering extra detail.
- If you do not remember, say you don’t remember rather than guessing.
- Don’t speculate — say “I don’t know” or “I don’t recall” and offer to provide documents that will refresh your memory.
- Practice direct, concise answers with your attorney. Rehearse likely lines of questioning.
- If a question is improper or calls for privileged information, consult your attorney before answering; they can object or instruct you not to answer.
- Maintain composure; do not argue with counsel or the judge.
- Ask to take a break if you feel overwhelmed.

7) Discovery and deposition-specific questions to clarify with your attorney
- Will I be deposed? If so, when and who will attend?
- What scope of questions can I expect in deposition vs. trial?
- Can you object during my deposition, and will you give me instructions not to answer certain questions?
- How should I handle misleading or compound questions?

8) Settlement & mediation considerations to discuss
- Is mediation recommended? When should it occur?
- What confidentiality protections apply during mediation?
- If settlement talks include financial or custody concessions, what are the short- and long-term implications?

9) Logistical questions to ask Maria the day before a hearing/trial
