Yes — I can help. Below I’ve organized practical things to ask Maria Santos, likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask at a hearing or trial, documents you should have ready, and short prep tips (how to answer, what to avoid). You can take this list to your next meeting with Maria or ask me to turn any part into a short email or a mock Q&A for practice.

A. First things to confirm with your attorney (procedural / case status)
- Exactly what is the upcoming hearing called and what is at stake there (case management conference, hearing on a motion, preliminary injunction, evidentiary hearing, settlement conference, etc.)?
- What are the specific issues we expect to cover at that hearing?
- What will the judge decide at that hearing vs what would be reserved for trial?
- Timeline to trial (estimated dates, deadlines for discovery, motions, pretrial conference).
- Local rules / courtroom procedures I should know for San Diego County civil division (filing format, exhibit handling, time limits, courtroom etiquette).
- What filings or evidence must be submitted in advance (exhibits, witness lists, declarations)?
- Are there any immediate deadlines I must meet (discovery responses, disclosures, protective order requests)?
- Are there any motions you recommend we file before the hearing (motion to dismiss, motion for summary judgment, motion in limine, motion for protective order, motion for preliminary injunction or to quash subpoena)?
- What are our settlement goals and parameters? Are we open to mediation / settlement now?
- Who will speak for us at the hearing — you, co-counsel, or me (if asked to testify)?
- Are there any confidentiality or sealing steps we should take for filings that contain financial/custody details?

B. Evidence, witness, and discovery questions to ask your attorney
- What documents and communications are strongest for proving or defending the NDA? (signed NDA, emails about scope, drafts, consideration, payments)
- What documents do they likely have that we should try to obtain via discovery (text messages, drafts, internal memos)?
- Who are the witnesses we will call or who they will call? Which witnesses’ testimony will be most important?
- Do we need declarations or affidavits for the hearing? Who should prepare them?
- Is a protective order or sealing motion appropriate given sensitive financial or custody information?
- What privilege concerns exist (attorney-client, work product)? Do we need a privilege log?
- Are there authenticity or chain-of-custody issues with electronic evidence? Do we need forensic copies?
- What damages or remedies are we seeking or defending against? How do we quantify them (actual damages, statutory, punitive, injunction)?

C. Questions about legal theory and likely defenses (ask Maria)
- Under California law, is the NDA enforceable as written? Any clauses that are overbroad, ambiguous, or void as against public policy?
- What defenses might the respondent raise (no agreement, lack of consideration, ambiguity, fraud, duress, waiver, impossibility, first amendment / public interest)?
- How will custody or financial matters interact with the NDA claims (e.g., alleged disclosure affecting custody evaluation, alleged misuse of funds tied to NDA breach)?
- What are the burdens of proof for the judge at the hearing and at trial (preponderance of evidence in civil matters)?
- What specific legal standards (elements) must we prove to show breach and recover remedies (injunction, damages, attorneys’ fees)?

D. Questions about what opposing counsel might ask you or your witnesses (prepare answers and strategy)
- Did you sign the NDA? When and under what circumstances?
- What did the NDA say (scope, duration, confidential information definition, permitted disclosures)?
- Did you intentionally disclose any information covered by the NDA? If so, when, how, and to whom? Why?
- What communications did you have with the respondent about the NDA before/after signing?
- What is your timeline of events? (Be ready to give a clear, chronologically ordered account.)
- Were there any changes or drafts of the NDA? Who prepared them?
- Were you paid or otherwise given consideration? If money or benefits were exchanged, show records.
- Did you take steps to keep information confidential (passwords, limited distribution, confidentiality labels)?
- Did you mitigate the alleged harm (notify the other side, try to retract, secure confidential documents)?
- Are there any third parties who received or witnessed disclosures? Who are they and what did they see/hear?
- How do the custody/financial facts relate to this dispute — were disclosures necessary for child welfare, court processes, or professional advice?

E. Questions the judge may ask at a hearing (short, direct)
- What relief are you requesting at this hearing?
- What facts can you point to right now to justify that relief?
- Have you produced relevant documents to opposing counsel? (This ties to discovery compliance.)
- What is the specific language in the NDA that you rely on?
- Why is an injunction (if requested) necessary and narrowly tailored?
- If opposing counsel raises public interest, how do you respond?
- What prejudice will the respondent face if relief is granted or denied?
- Have you attempted to resolve this without court intervention?

F. Practical prep and sample short answers / tips for testimony
- Always be truthful, never guess. If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” and offer to check the document.
- Keep answers short and fact-based. Avoid volunteering extra information.
- Practice a concise chronology of events (dates, places, communications).
- For each factual claim, know which document or witness supports it.
- Avoid argumentative or speculative statements (e.g., don’t state motives you can’t prove).
- If asked for legal conclusions, defer to your lawyer: “I’ll let my attorney address legal questions.”

G. Documents and information checklist to bring to a prep meeting
- The fully executed NDA (all versions, including drafts and redlines).
- All related communications: emails, text messages, letters, messaging apps, meeting notes.
- Any payment records, invoices, proof of consideration or performance tied to the NDA.
- List of witnesses (contact info), with a short note on what each observed/knows.
- Timeline (one-page chronology of events with dates).
- Any court orders or filings already in the case (complaints, answers, motions).
- Financial records you mention as relevant (bank statements, tax returns, expense logs).
- Custody orders or other family-law documents that might interact with the dispute.
- Documents showing steps taken to keep information confidential (ND labels, storage access logs).
- Any prior settlement offers or communications about settlement.

H. Strategy items to discuss with Maria
- Mock examination / witness prep session — can we rehearse likely cross-examination?
- Which witnesses should be prepared and who should be deposed first?
- Protective order / sealing strategy for sensitive exhibits; redaction procedures.
- Evidence authentication plan for electronic messages and metadata.
- Whether to pursue or oppose early injunctions or expedited discovery.
- Motion strategy: motion to compel discovery, motions in limine, summary judgment possibilities.
- Settlement negotiation plan and acceptable ranges / walk-away points.

I. Things to ask Maria to do before the hearing
- Identify the top 5 facts we must prove at the hearing and the evidence for each.
- Draft any necessary declarations and identify signers.
- File any requested protective orders or sealing motions.
- Provide a short script for me for answering common judge/opposing counsel questions.
- Schedule a mock Q&A/cross-examination practice session.

J. Final practical tips for the hearing or depositions
- Dress conservatively and arrive early.
- Bring organized, labeled exhibit binders and a one-page chronology.
- Keep your phone off; do not discuss the case with anyone except your attorney.
- If opposing counsel asks you a question outside your knowledge, ask to confer with your attorney.
- Record names and details of any new points raised that you need to follow up on.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short email for you to send to Maria listing these questions and requesting a mock prep session.
- Turn the “Questions opposing counsel/judge may ask” into a mock Q&A for practice.
- Help you build the one-page chronology or an exhibit list from the documents you have.

Which of those would you like me to do next?

