Good — I can help you get organized and ready. Below I’ve put together (A) a focused list of questions you should ask Maria Santos about what the judge or opposing counsel might ask, (B) likely questions you should be prepared to answer from the judge and from opposing counsel, (C) a short document/evidence checklist, and (D) practical prep actions and next steps. You can bring this list to your meeting or ask Maria to run a mock Q&A with you.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & procedure
  - What specifically will happen at the preliminary hearing/case management conference in San Diego County civil division? What issues will the judge expect us to address?
  - What are the key deadlines and procedural risks between now and a potential trial?
  - Are there any local rules or judge-specific practices I should know about?

- Legal theory and strategy
  - How does the opponent characterize the nondisclosure agreement (NDA) dispute and what is our strongest legal theory?
  - What defenses or counterclaims should I expect the respondent to raise?
  - Under what circumstances would you recommend settlement versus proceeding to trial?

- Questions the judge might ask (ask Maria to prepare me for)
  - What questions should I expect from the judge at the preliminary hearing? (e.g., scope of the dispute, status of discovery, requests for scheduling, custody/financial impact)
  - How should I answer when the judge asks about the factual basis for the NDA claim or about the requested relief?

- Questions opposing counsel might ask (ask Maria to prepare me for)
  - What hostile questions am I likely to face in depositions or at trial about the NDA, finances, or custody?
  - How should I handle questions that try to expand the dispute beyond the NDA (e.g., personal matters, prior communications)?

- Evidence & witnesses
  - What documents and communications are essential to prove our position (signed NDA, emails, text messages, bank records, timelines)?
  - Do we need any expert witnesses (forensic accountant, custody expert) and when should we retain them?
  - Which witnesses will opposing counsel likely call and what attacks should I expect on their credibility?

- Discovery & motions
  - What discovery requests should we serve and which ones should I anticipate from the respondent?
  - Should we consider (or prepare) any immediate motions (protective order, motion to compel, motion for sanctions, motion to dismiss, motion to seal)?
  - How do we handle privileged or confidential documents and can we request an in-camera review or a protective order?

- Custody & financial implications
  - How might custody or financial details be used by the respondent to weaken our position, and how can we prepare to neutralize that?
  - Are there ways to limit disclosure of sensitive custody/financial information while still meeting discovery obligations?

- Risk management & settlement
  - What are realistic outcomes (damages, injunctive relief, attorneys’ fees)? What is our bottom line for settlement?
  - What are potential costs and timelines to trial?

- Communication & logistics
  - How do you want me to communicate with you and what materials do you want before the hearing?
  - Would you conduct a practice session or deposition prep with me? If so, when?

B. Questions you should prepare to answer (judge and opposing counsel)
Likely judge questions at preliminary hearing / case management
- What are the key issues to be decided in this case?
- What is the status of discovery? Are there outstanding discovery disputes?
- Do you request any special management (expedited schedule, bifurcation, sealing)?
- Are parties ready to consider mediation or alternative dispute resolution?
- Are there any custody or financial issues that require protective measures or confidentiality orders?

Likely opposing counsel / deposition questions (hostile lines you should prep for)
- Please describe your relationship/communications with the respondent that led to the NDA.
- Did you sign the NDA voluntarily? Were you represented by counsel at the time?
- What specific confidential information is covered by the NDA and how was it disclosed or allegedly breached?
- Can you identify the documents/emails/texts that support your claim? (Be ready to produce and explain)
- Have you shared the allegedly confidential information with anyone else? If so, why?
- What damages or harm have you suffered from the alleged breach? How are damages calculated?
- Questions about finances: income, bank transfers, financial records, alleged losses (be prepared with how this is relevant to the claim).
- Questions about custody: timelines, living arrangements, child support, custody arrangements (if relevant) — opposing counsel may probe to show bias, motive, or credibility issues.
- Questions designed to impeach your credibility: inconsistencies in your timeline, missing documents, social media posts, prior statements.

C. Documents and evidence checklist to prepare now
- Fully executed NDA(s) (all versions and dates)
- All communications about the NDA: emails, text messages, messaging apps, letters, notes
- Timeline of events (chronological, concise with dates and brief descriptions)
- Financial records tied to the dispute: bank statements, invoices, receipts, accounting records, payments or transfers
- Custody documents or orders (if relevant): court orders, custody agreements, communications about custody arrangements
- Logs of disclosures (if you documented when/what confidential info was shared)
- Any documents showing harm/damages: lost contracts, lost revenue, valuation, screenshots, witness statements
- Metadata and original electronic files where possible (preserve originals)
- Notes of conversations (dates, participants) and any contemporaneous notes you made
- Social media or public posts that might be used by opposing counsel (identify anything that could harm credibility)
- Privilege log: list of documents you believe are privileged (do not inadvertently waive privilege)

D. Practical prep, preservation, and conduct
- Preservation: Immediately preserve all relevant documents and devices. Do not delete anything. Consider a written litigation hold notification for anyone in your control.
- Avoid contact: Do not discuss the case with the respondent or post about it on social media. Direct others to give all requests to your attorney.
- Practice short, plain answers: Judges and opposing counsel expect concise, factual answers — avoid volunteering extra information.
- Deposition / court testimony: rehearse potential hostile questions; practice answering clearly, slowly, and sticking to the facts.
- Privilege: Ask Maria when you should assert privilege and how to maintain privilege in discovery.
- Protective orders and sealing: Ask Maria whether to seek a protective order for custody/financial records or to file motions to seal.
- Mock examination: Ask Maria to role-play judge questioning and opposing counsel cross-examination.
- Logistics: Confirm hearing date/time, courtroom, required filings, length of hearing, and what you should bring/wear.

E. Suggested immediate questions for your first phone/email to Maria (short list you can use)
- Can we schedule a prep session for the preliminary hearing and a mock deposition?
- What specific documents do you need from me this week?
- Do you recommend we file any protective motions before discovery begins?
- What is your recommended settlement strategy and current valuation of potential remedies?

F. Final practical tips
- Keep answers factual and avoid speculation. If you don’t remember, say so and offer to provide documentation later.
- Label and organize documents so you can quickly locate an item if asked.
- Ask Maria to tell you which facts are most likely to be attacked and to give you a short script for those topics.

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn this into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your meeting with Maria.
- Draft a short email to Maria asking to schedule hearing prep and listing the documents you’ll bring.

Tell me which of those you want (checklist or an email draft), and if you want the materials tailored to any specific facts (dates, particular disclosures, or custody details).

