Good — this is the right time to be organized. Below I’ve put together a focused checklist you can use in a meeting with Maria Santos and to practice for the hearing. It’s grouped so you can quickly find things to ask your attorney, likely questions from the judge and opposing counsel, what to prepare and bring, and how to practice answers.

Quick initial question for you
- Can you confirm what type of “preliminary hearing” this is: a case-management conference / initial case conference, a preliminary injunction hearing, a hearing on a temporary restraining order, or something else? The likely questions and preparation differ by type.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status and process
  - What exactly will happen at the upcoming hearing and what is our objective there?
  - Is the court hearing a case-management conference, preliminary injunction hearing, TRO hearing, or something else?
  - Is this a bench or jury matter? Which judge or department is assigned and are there any standing orders I should know about?
- Procedural posture & timeline
  - What are the next deadlines (discovery cut-off, motion filing deadlines, mediation, trial date estimate)?
  - What motions do you recommend now (motions in limine, motion to dismiss, summary judgment, motion for protective order, application for injunctive relief)?
- Substance of the NDA dispute
  - What are the key legal issues we expect to litigate (enforceability of the NDA, scope, trade secret vs. confidential info, breach, damages, invalid defenses)?
  - What is our theory of damages and what evidence will we need to prove them?
  - Are there any special statutes or California rules that are particularly relevant (e.g., trade secret protections, California contract law)?
- Evidence & discovery
  - What documents, devices, emails, communications, etc., should I locate and preserve now?
  - Do we need forensic images of devices or third-party subpoenas?
  - Should we seek a protective order or designate materials “confidential” in discovery?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Will I be required to testify at the hearing or trial? What should I expect to be asked?
  - Which witnesses will we call and what are the key points they should be prepared to make?
  - Should we depose any opposing witnesses before the hearing?
- Custody & financial matters
  - How could custody or financial circumstances be used by the respondent or the judge? What documentation will be relevant (bank records, tax returns, child support orders)?
  - How should I handle questions about my finances or custody in court — what should I disclose and what should I avoid saying without counsel present?
- Settlement & strategy
  - What settlement options should I consider and what are the risks/benefits?
  - If the other side offers a settlement, who will negotiate and what authority should I give you?
- Practical & courtroom conduct
  - How should I behave and respond in court (tone, brevity, avoiding volunteered information)?
  - Do you recommend I prepare a one-page factual timeline or summary for the judge and for you to use?
- Fees & costs
  - What is our budget for discovery/trial? How will fees be billed and what costs should I expect before the next stage?
- Risk & appeals
  - What are my exposure and worst-case outcomes? What are the likely appellate issues if we lose?

Likely questions the judge might ask (by hearing type)
- Case-management / scheduling conference
  - Have the parties been properly served? Are there any jurisdictional issues?
  - What is the status of discovery and what is the proposed schedule to trial?
  - Are the parties ready for mediation/alternative dispute resolution?
  - Any anticipated motions or need for special discovery?
- Preliminary injunction / TRO hearing
  - What specific conduct do you ask the court to enjoin?
  - Is there a likelihood of irreparable harm and why is money damages insufficient?
  - What is the balance of hardships and the public interest?
  - What evidence demonstrates immediate risk and how quickly do you need relief?
- Evidentiary or motion hearings
  - Which documents are admitted and have they been authenticated?
  - Are any communications privileged? Has privilege been asserted properly?
  - Are there disputes about scope of discovery or spoliation of evidence?

Likely questions opposing counsel will ask you (and how to prepare)
- Factual timeline and specifics
  - When did the disclosure occur, what exactly was disclosed, to whom, and why?
  - Who had access to the information and what steps did you take to keep it confidential?
  - Were there any authorizations to share the information or prior disclosures that became public?
- Contract and intent questions
  - Did you sign the NDA? When and under what circumstances? Were any changes negotiated?
  - How did you interpret specific clauses (scope, duration, permitted disclosures)?
  - Did you intend to breach the NDA? Were you negligent or reckless?
- Damages and financials
  - What actual losses resulted from the alleged disclosure? Can you show specific financial harm?
  - Did you mitigate damages? Any alternative explanations for the plaintiff’s losses?
- Custody and motive probing (if relevant)
  - Are custody or financial pressures relevant to motive — e.g., did financial need or custody dispute influence behavior?
  - Personal background questions that could be used to impeach credibility.
- Prior statements & social media
  - Have you made inconsistent statements previously (emails, texts, social posts)?
  - Can you authenticate emailed/digital evidence or will opposing counsel try to challenge authenticity?

How to prepare and what to bring (documents & exhibits)
- The executed NDA(s), including any amendments, attachments, or related agreements
- All communications related to the issue: emails, texts, instant messages, meeting notes, draft agreements
- A clear chronological timeline (one-page) of events with dates and key actions
- Logs or lists of persons who received the information and when
- Financial documents tied to damages: bank records, invoices, profit/loss statements, tax returns (if relevant)
- Custody-related court orders or agreements if they bear on credibility or financial circumstances
- Device lists and any forensic reports or preservation evidence
- Witness contact list and short witness statements or summaries
- Copies for the judge, opposing counsel, and your attorney of key exhibits (multiple copies)
- Proposed protective order or confidentiality designations (if you need them)

Evidentiary and privilege issues to ask Maria about
- Which communications are protected by attorney-client privilege or work product?
- How to authenticate electronic evidence and preserve metadata?
- Whether to seek a protective order for confidential business information
- How opposing counsel may attempt to admit hearsay and how you should respond

Practical guidance for answering questions in court or deposition
- Be concise: answer only the question asked; don’t volunteer extra detail.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.
- Ask for clarification if you don’t understand a question.
- Avoid speculation: don’t hypothesize about others’ motives or facts you don’t know.
- If a question would require disclosure of privileged communications, tell your attorney immediately — do not answer until advised.
- Coordinate responses with Maria beforehand and rehearse answers to tough questions.

Preparation steps (action items)
- Confirm the type of hearing and any judge-specific rules/standing orders.
- Gather and preserve all documents and devices; create a central, labeled exhibit binder.
- Meet with Maria to set the hearing strategy and review the short one-page timeline.
- Do a mock Q&A with Maria or with me (I can draft practice questions/responses if you want).
- Identify and prepare any witnesses and obtain short witness summaries.
- Ask Maria whether to propose a protective order and to draft it if needed.
- Make sure all discovery holds are in place to avoid spoliation.

Sample, concise answers to practice (examples)
- Q: “When did you first realize the information was disclosed?”
  A: “I first learned about it on [date].”
- Q: “Why did you share this information with X?”
  A: “I did not intend to violate the agreement. I believed [brief factual reason].”
- Q: “Do you have copies of the NDA and communications?”
  A: “Yes. I’ve provided them to my counsel and brought copies today.”

Red flags to discuss with your attorney immediately
- Any deleted or altered messages or missing devices — potential spoliation risk
- Inconsistent written statements or social media posts
- Any prior agreements that conflict with the NDA
- Any communications that suggest intent to breach or to conceal disclosure

Offer to help
- I can draft a one-page timeline/summary you can bring to the hearing.
- I can create a tailored list of practice Q&A based on the exact facts you provide.
- I can prepare a short script/answers for the most likely questions to rehearse with Maria.

Next step
- Tell me which type of preliminary hearing this is (case management vs. preliminary injunction vs. TRO vs. other) and any key dates or the judge’s name, and I’ll draft a one-page hearing brief and a 20-question practice Q&A you can rehearse with Maria.

Would you like me to draft the one-page timeline and a practice-question list now? If so, please share a concise chronology of the main events (dates, who, what happened) and the exact relief you or the other side are seeking.

