Good — this is exactly the kind of organizing that helps make hearings and trials less stressful. Below I’ve put together (1) focused questions you should ask your attorney, (2) the kinds of questions a judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask (with short notes about how to prepare), (3) evidence/documents to gather, and (4) practical prep and courtroom tips. I avoided giving legal advice — use these to guide your conversation with Maria Santos at Santos Law Office.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case strategy & goals
  - What is our legal theory and the remedies we will ask the court for (injunctive relief, damages, declaration)?
  - What is your recommended short-term objective for the upcoming hearing (e.g., preliminary injunction, case management, discovery schedule)?
- Procedural clarification
  - Is the upcoming event a preliminary injunction hearing, case management conference, or something else? What should I expect?
  - What are local San Diego County deadlines and rules I must meet (disclosures, exhibit lists, filings)?
- Opposing party / likely defenses
  - What defenses is the respondent likely to raise (e.g., NDA unenforceable, waiver, lack of breach, statute of limitations, First Amendment)?
  - How might they use my financial or custody information? Can we limit that through motions or protective orders?
- Evidence & witnesses
  - What specific evidence will be most persuasive to the judge? Which documents do you need from me right away?
  - Who should we plan to call as witnesses? Will depositions be necessary, and when?
- Discovery & confidentiality
  - Should we file a protective order or motion in limine to keep sensitive financial/custody info out of public record?
  - How do we handle subpoenas or requests that seek personal/custody-related material?
- Settlement & costs
  - What are reasonable settlement options and the trade-offs (confidentiality, financial terms)?
  - What are the likely costs (time, attorney fees) and an estimated timeline to resolution or trial?
- Preparation & communication
  - Can we do a mock Q&A or deposition prep session? How long before the hearing?
  - What is the best way to communicate with you between now and the hearing? Are there any topics I should not discuss with anyone else (especially the respondent)?
- Courtroom logistics
  - What should I bring to the hearing? How should I dress? How long will it likely take?
  - If the respondent questions me directly or the judge asks something unexpected, how should I respond?

2) Questions the judge might ask (and how to prepare)
Note: tailor answers with your attorney’s input; these are the types of judicial questions to expect at early hearings.

- “What relief are you asking the court to grant?”
  - Prepare a concise statement of requested relief and the legal basis.
- “What facts support your claim that the NDA was breached?”
  - Have a clear timeline, key documents, and one- or two-sentence summary for each alleged breach.
- “Why is injunctive relief necessary/why not just monetary damages?”
  - If seeking injunction, be ready to explain irreparable harm and lack of adequate remedy at law.
- “Have you mitigated damages? What steps did you take?”
  - Be prepared to list corrective actions you took (e.g., revoking access, notifying parties).
- “Are there competing claims or legal/constitutional issues?”
  - Work with your attorney to anticipate and respond to free-speech or public-policy arguments.
- “What discovery remains and how long will it take?”
  - Know your proposed discovery schedule and what’s outstanding.
- Procedural questions (standing, jurisdiction, timeliness, pleadings)
  - Make sure you can state succinctly why the court has authority and why the claim is timely.

3) Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask (and how to prepare)
Opposing counsel will try to undermine enforceability, relevance, and damages.

- About the NDA itself:
  - “Who drafted the NDA?” “Was it signed voluntarily?” “What consideration was given?”
  - Prepare background: copy of signed NDA, execution dates, any negotiations or drafts.
- About the alleged disclosure:
  - “What exactly was disclosed, when, and to whom?” “Do you have proof (emails, files, witness testimony)?”
  - Have concrete examples, timestamps, recipients, and corroborating documents.
- About intent and awareness:
  - “Did you ever authorize the disclosure? Did you or others share similar info elsewhere?”
  - Be ready to explain confidentiality measures you used and any prior disclosures.
- About damages and causation:
  - “What actual harm or losses did you suffer because of this?” “How did you calculate damages?”
  - Provide documentation of financial harm or business impact, and be honest about estimates.
- About custody/financial matters:
  - “How do your personal finances or custody situation relate to the claims?” “Is this relevant to credibility or harm?”
  - Discuss with Maria what is truly relevant; preserve privacy via protective orders.
- Impeachment-style questions:
  - Expect attempts to highlight inconsistencies. Review your timeline and prior statements so you don’t contradict yourself.

4) Documents and evidence to collect now
- Signed NDA(s), all versions and drafts, amendments
- All communications (emails, texts, DMs) with the respondent around the time of the alleged breach
- Copies/screenshots of the disclosed material (with metadata if possible)
- Access logs, file-sharing records, timestamps, device logs
- Financial records showing alleged damages or mitigation costs (invoices, lost revenue, valuation)
- Records showing confidentiality measures (passwords, nondisclosure markings, limited distribution lists)
- Any prior public disclosures or prior inconsistent statements
- Witness contact details and a short summary of what each witness can verify
- Court and filing documents already submitted (complaint, declarations, exhibits)
Keep originals and a clear indexed chronology.

5) Motions and protective steps to discuss with your attorney
- Motion for protective order to limit disclosure of sensitive custody/financial facts
- Motion in limine to exclude irrelevant or prejudicial evidence at trial
- Motion for temporary restraining order / preliminary injunction (if immediate harm)
- Requests for expedited discovery if evidence may be destroyed
- Sanctions or spoliation notices if records were deleted intentionally

6) How custody or financial details might be used and how to protect them
- Opposing counsel may try to use custody/finances to attack credibility or motive. Ask Maria whether those facts are:
  - Directly relevant to the NDA claim (if not, ask to move to exclude)
  - Properly redacted or handled under a protective order
- Ask about sealing filings or presenting sensitive testimony in camera (private) or under limited disclosure.

7) Practical preparation & courtroom behavior
- Do a mock Q&A with Maria (role-play both friendly and aggressive lines of questioning).
- Practice concise answers — one- or two-sentence responses; don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t remember details, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing; confirm with documents when possible.
- Pause before answering; take notes; consult your attorney when appropriate.
- Bring organized binder of exhibits and a short one-page timeline to the hearing.
- Dress professionally, arrive early, and turn off devices.

8) Short checklist / next steps to confirm with Maria
- Confirm the type of hearing and the concrete goal for it.
- Set a deadline for producing requested documents to your attorney.
- Schedule a mock Q&A / deposition prep session at least a few days before the hearing.
- Ask Maria to prepare a proposed order, witness list, exhibit list, and any protective order drafts.
- Agree on communication rules with opposing counsel and what to do if contacted directly.

9) Sample short questions you can say to Maria in your meeting
- “Can you walk me through, in one sentence, our core argument and what we’ll ask the judge for at the hearing?”
- “What precise documents do you need from me by [date]?”
- “What are the top three weaknesses the respondent is likely to attack, and how should I answer questions about them?”
- “How should I handle questions about my custody situation if the respondent brings it up?”
- “Can we do a 60-minute mock where you ask the tough questions I’ll likely face?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email to Maria Santos listing these questions and requesting a prep session, or
- Create a printable one-page hearing prep sheet you can bring to the meeting?

Tell me which and I’ll draft it for you.

