I can help — below is a structured checklist you can use to prepare for your preliminary hearing and possible trial, plus suggested questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos) about what the judge or opposing counsel might ask. If you want, I can also draft an email to Maria with this list.

Quick clarification before we go further
- When you say “preliminary hearing,” do you mean a hearing on a temporary restraining order/preliminary injunction, a case-management conference, or something else in the civil division? Knowing that will let me tailor the questions precisely.

What to ask your attorney — high priority questions
1. Case posture and likely questions
- What is the exact legal theory we are pursuing (breach of contract, misappropriation of trade secrets, tort claims, etc.) and how will that shape what the judge will ask?
- At the preliminary hearing, what are the specific legal issues the judge will decide?
- What questions do you expect opposing counsel to ask me on the stand? (Please list likely lines of attack.)
- What questions might the judge ask me directly?

2. Evidence and documents
- Which documents should I absolutely bring to the hearing/trial (original NDA, any signed amendments, emails/texts, drafts, meeting notes, invoices, bank records, file metadata)?
- Are there documents we should subpoena or request in discovery from the respondent?
- How should we authenticate electronic evidence (emails, metadata)? Do we need IT or forensic support?
- Can we get a protective order for sensitive financial or custody-related materials? How do we propose redactions/exhibits?

3. Witnesses and testimony
- Which witnesses should I expect to testify for us and for the respondent?
- Do any witnesses need preparation or rehearsals? What will the cross-examination focus on?
- Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., damages expert, forensic analyst)? If so, by when must they be disclosed?

4. Potential cross-examination topics (financial & custody)
- How might my finances or custody situation be used against me, and how should I respond?
- Are there financial documents the respondent might request that are irrelevant or should be protected?
- If custody issues are raised, how do we limit irrelevant, prejudicial inquiries? (Request motions in limine, protective orders.)

5. Legal defenses & weaknesses
- What are the strengths and weaknesses of our case?
- What are the respondent’s most likely defenses and how should I prepare to address them?
- Are there legal motions we should file before the hearing/trial (motion to dismiss, summary judgment, motion in limine)?

6. Strategy, settlement, and costs
- What is our litigation strategy for the hearing and for trial (narrow to discrete issues vs. broad discovery)?
- What is your view on settlement? What minimum/maximum settlement terms would be acceptable?
- What are the estimated costs and timeline to trial versus settlement?

7. Logistics & courtroom conduct
- Where/when is the hearing? Any local San Diego County civil division procedures or courtroom rules I should know?
- Will the hearing be in front of a judge or a jury (pretrial bench vs. jury trial)?
- How long will I be on the stand? What are the courtroom procedures for exhibits and evidence?
- What should I say or avoid saying in court or in communications with opposing counsel?

8. Communication and next steps
- How should I communicate with you between now and the hearing (phone, email)? Who else on your team will handle parts of this?
- What do you want me to do this week (gather documents, prepare a timeline, write a memory of events)?

Practical preparation checklist (documents & items to bring)
- Complete and signed NDA and any related agreements or amendments
- All email/text/message threads referencing the NDA or the disputed communications (exported with timestamps if possible)
- Timeline/chronology of events (one-page summary + detailed timeline)
- Financial records that are relevant (payments, invoices, bank statements, records of alleged damages)
- Custody-related orders or documents that may be referenced (court orders, parenting plans) — but check with Maria before bringing very sensitive custody files into public filings
- Witness contact information and short summaries of their expected testimony
- Any prior litigation or settlement communications (demand letters, cease-and-desist, settlement offers)
- Proposed exhibits list and copies (organized and numbered)
- Notebook with short bullet answers to likely personal questions about dates, conversations, and actions

Sample questions opposing counsel or judge might ask you (practice answers)
- “When exactly did you sign the NDA?” — Be precise with dates and refer to the document.
- “What did the NDA require you to keep confidential?” — Read the clause in plain terms and explain how it applied.
- “Did you ever authorize sharing this information?” — Answer Yes/No and provide supporting facts; don’t guess.
- “What damages did you suffer from the alleged breach?” — Give a concise summary and refer to supporting documents.
- “Why should the Court grant the relief you seek at this preliminary stage?” — State legal standard briefly (your counsel should prepare the precise legal standard) and the specific facts that meet it.
- If asked about finances/custody: “How might your financial/custody situation affect this case?” — Be factual, concise, and let counsel handle linking relevance.

How to answer questions on the stand (best practices)
- Pause and breathe before answering.
- Answer only the question asked; don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.
- If you need to refer to a document, ask permission to look at it and then answer.
- Keep answers short and factual; avoid arguing with the questioner.

Procedural items to confirm with Maria
- Deadlines for discovery, witness/exhibit lists, and expert disclosures in San Diego County
- Whether we need to file any emergency motions or requests for expedited discovery
- Motion practice timeline (motions in limine, summary judgment)
- Whether to seek a protective order before producing financial/custody documents
- Local rules or judge-specific preferences for exhibit format, electronic filing, or remote appearances

Requests you can make of your attorney now
- A plain-language one-page “what to expect” script for the hearing and a 2–3 page list of likely Q&A for your testimony
- Mock direct and cross-examination practice sessions
- A prioritized “bring to hearing” packet prepared by counsel (what you should bring)
- A confidentiality/protective order motion drafted if necessary

Next steps I can help with right now
- Tailor this checklist to the specific type of preliminary hearing if you tell me which hearing it is.
- Draft an email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and requesting specific guidance or a prep session.
- Create a one-page witness/evidence timeline or a mock Q&A script you can practice.

