I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Quick note: I’m not a lawyer, so this is general preparation help, not legal advice. Use these suggestions to guide a focused conversation with Maria Santos at Santos Law Office.

1) Key goals for your meeting with Maria
- Confirm the legal theory: exactly what claim(s) you’re asserting (breach of NDA? tort? declaratory relief?) and what respondent is likely to counter with.
- Identify the critical facts and documents that will prove each element of your claim or the respondent’s defenses.
- Build a practical plan for the preliminary hearing (what the judge will want to know) and the likely path to trial (discovery, motions, timeline, settlement posture).

2) Questions to ask Maria (to understand what the judge or opposing counsel might ask)
- About the case and procedure
  - What will the judge expect at the preliminary hearing in Civil Division (San Diego County)? What issues are typically decided then?
  - What is the likely schedule from preliminary hearing to trial (discovery deadlines, motion cutoffs, trial estimate)?
  - What standard of proof applies and which legal elements must we prove?
- About what opposing counsel will ask
  - What factual points will opposing counsel focus on to weaken our claim (intent, notice, scope of the NDA, consent, public knowledge, independent development)?
  - What lines of questioning should I expect about my motives, communications, and timeline of events?
  - How will they attack credibility (inconsistencies, prior statements, missing documents) and how can we mitigate that?
- About what the judge will ask
  - What will the judge want to know at the preliminary hearing (issues of jurisdiction, service, status of discovery, any emergency relief, whether settlement talks occurred)?
  - If confidentiality is central, how will the court handle requests to disclose sensitive materials? How likely is a sealing order or protective order?
- About evidence & witnesses
  - What documents are essential and why? (NDA signed version(s), email chains, drafts, contemporaneous notes, billing/financial records)
  - Which witnesses will be most persuasive and what will they be asked?
  - Do we need an expert (e.g., on damages or forensics)?
- About custody/financial matters that may intersect
  - Could custody or financial circumstances be used against us? What is the proper way to introduce or resist introduction of those topics?
  - Should we seek to keep any custody/financial records sealed or subject to a protective order?
- Discovery & privilege
  - What specific discovery will the respondent likely request (documents, interrogatories, depositions)?
  - How do we handle privileged communications or work product? What should we log on a privilege log?
  - Are there strategies to narrow or limit overly broad discovery requests?
- Settlement & strategy
  - What settlement options are realistic at this stage? What are pros/cons of settlement versus going to trial?
  - What is our best and worst case scenario (damages, injunctive relief, costs/fees)?
  - Cost estimates: projected attorney fees and litigation expenses to trial.
- Hearing/trial prep for you personally
  - How should I prepare for direct and cross-examination? Can we do a mock Q&A?
  - What courtroom procedures or etiquette should I know for San Diego civil court?

3) Questions the judge might ask (be ready to answer concisely)
- What relief are you seeking and what facts support that relief?
- Has the respondent been properly served? Is there any jurisdictional issue?
- Are there outstanding discovery disputes or pending motions the court should know about?
- Why is confidentiality necessary here — what harm will occur if disclosure happens?
- Is the case likely to settle? Do the parties need mediation?
- Timeline: are the parties ready for a trial date? Are more discovery or expert reports needed?

4) Questions opposing counsel might ask you (examples to prepare short factual answers for)
- Who signed the NDA and when? Were there any subsequent amendments?
- How and when did the alleged disclosure happen? What exactly was disclosed?
- Did you or anyone else give consent to the disclosure?
- Were any efforts made to mitigate harm after the disclosure? (e.g., recall, notification)
- What specific damages or losses resulted from the disclosure? How are those calculated?
- Do you have communications that contradict your present account? (emails, drafts, texts)
- Any financial motive or personal conflict that might explain your conduct?

5) Documents and evidence checklist to gather now
- Final signed NDA and any prior drafts or related agreements
- All communications (emails, texts, DMs) with respondent and third parties relating to the NDA or the disclosed information — preserve originals and metadata if possible
- Timeline document: clear chronology of relevant events (dates, times, actions)
- Documents showing damages or mitigation (financial records, contracts, leaks, screenshots)
- Custody or financial records that may be relevant — ask Maria which ones to bring and whether to produce or file under seal
- Witness contact information and short witness statements
- Any prior legal filings, notices, or correspondence between counsel

6) Discovery & confidentiality protections to discuss
- Protective order: scope, confidentiality designations, procedures for contested documents
- Motion to seal or in camera review for extremely sensitive materials (when appropriate)
- Privilege logs and preserving attorney-client/work product protections
- Electronically stored information: how to preserve chain of custody and metadata

7) How to prepare for questioning
- Keep answers short and factual; don’t offer extra information beyond the question.
- Practice common questions aloud with Maria (or do a mock deposition).
- Review your timeline and documents until you can cite dates and specifics confidently.
- Be honest about uncertainties — “I don’t recall” is better than guessing.
- Stay calm and avoid arguing with counsel in front of the judge.

8) Practical courtroom and logistics questions to ask Maria
- Where and when is the preliminary hearing? Any documents we must file beforehand?
- Will I testify at the preliminary hearing or will Maria present arguments instead?
- What should I bring to court that day (exhibits, printed timeline, copies for the judge/opposing counsel)?
- Dress code and expected arrival time; how long do hearings usually last?

9) Next steps I recommend you take before meeting Maria
- Compile the documents above in a single folder (electronic + printed), with a one-page timeline.
- Make a short “top 5 facts” list that you want Maria to emphasize.
- Decide whether you want to pursue settlement talks before the preliminary hearing and ask Maria to prepare a negotiation memo.
- Ask Maria for a pre-hearing prep session (mock Q&A) if possible.

10) Offer to help further
- I can draft a concise email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and requesting a prep meeting or list of specific documents to bring. Would you like me to draft that now? If yes, tell me: preferred meeting date/time options and whether you want the document checklist attached.

If you want, I can also convert the above into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your meeting. Which would you prefer?

