Good — this is a smart place to start. Below I’ve organized practical, attorney-focused questions you can ask Maria Santos, plus likely lines of questioning a judge or opposing counsel may use, documents/evidence to prepare, and short prep tips so you’re ready at the preliminary hearing and beyond.

Before we start: do you want these as a checklist to bring to your meeting with Maria, or would you like me to draft a short email to Maria listing these items and asking for a meeting?

1) Key clarifying questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- What is the current posture of the case in the civil division (exactly what will the preliminary hearing address)?
- Based on our facts, what legal theories will the court consider for the NDA claim and any counterclaims or defenses?
- Who will be the plaintiff/petitioner and who is the respondent in the pleadings (confirm roles)?
- What is the standard of proof the judge will use at the hearing vs. trial?
- What specific questions do you expect the judge to ask me at the preliminary hearing?
- What questions or lines of attack do you expect opposing counsel to use on me and on any witnesses?
- Which documents and communications will be most important to prove the NDA’s terms, breach, damages, and my mitigation efforts?
- Do we have to file any pre-hearing briefs, declarations, exhibit lists, or proposed orders? Deadlines?
- What discovery remains, and are there imminent discovery actions (depositions, subpoenas, motions to compel)?
- Should we ask for or expect a protective order or sealing order for sensitive material (custody/financial information)?
- Could custody or financial details shift venue, affect admissibility, or change our strategy? If so, how?
- What is our settlement posture and range? Are there court-sponsored settlement options?
- What are the risks at the hearing (sanctions, adverse findings, interim relief) and worst-case outcomes?
- What testimony do you want me to prepare to give, and how should I practice my answers?
- What communications should I avoid (social media, discussing the case with certain people)?
- If the case goes to trial, what is the likely timeline and what additional evidence or experts will be needed?

2) Questions the judge may ask (preliminary hearing / status hearing)
- What are the claims and what relief are you seeking?
- Do you have the original/signed NDA or an admissible copy?
- When and how was the NDA communicated/served?
- What specific provisions of the NDA do you allege were breached?
- Who are the parties and what were their respective roles/authorities under the agreement?
- What evidence supports your claim of breach (dates, communications, witnesses)?
- What damages or harm have you suffered and how were they calculated?
- Have you made any efforts to mitigate damages (stop the disclosure, notify the other party)?
- Have there been any attempts at settlement or mediation?
- What are the outstanding discovery or evidentiary issues that could affect trial readiness?
- Are there any confidentiality or sealing requests necessary for exhibits?
- If custody/financial issues are relevant, how do you propose they be handled procedurally?

3) Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask you (lines that can undermine your case)
- When did you first sign/receive the NDA? Can you produce the original?
- How and when did you notify the respondent of the confidential nature of the material?
- Did the respondent have a prior relationship or preexisting right to the information?
- Did you ever waive confidentiality (verbal permission, written waiver, public disclosure)?
- Are there inconsistencies in your timeline or story — can you explain specific date/time discrepancies?
- What motive would you have to claim harm (financial incentive, custody leverage)?
- Did you alter or fail to preserve relevant documents or communications?
- Can you identify witnesses who will corroborate your account? Have they been contacted/prepared?
- What steps did you take immediately after discovering the alleged disclosure?
- If damages are claimed: show receipts, invoices, valuations, calculations — how were these derived?

4) Documents and evidence to gather and bring to your attorney meeting
- The signed NDA (original if possible) and any drafts, amendments, or side agreements
- All communications related to the NDA and the alleged disclosure (emails, texts, DMs, letters) in chronological order
- Dates/times and descriptions of specific alleged breaches (who said/did what, when)
- Any notices you sent (demand letters, cease-and-desist, preservation notices)
- Financial records showing claimed damages (invoices, contracts, lost business docs, bank statements)
- Custody or family court orders or filings, if they’re factually related or could be used by opponent
- Witness list with contact info and short summary of what each witness can confirm
- Any prior settlement negotiations or mediation notes
- Logs or metadata (file access logs, screenshots) if digital disclosure is alleged
- Social media posts or public statements relevant to confidentiality
- Any prior communications showing waiver, consent, or inconsistent positions

5) Practical preparation and testimony tips (how to behave at hearing/trial)
- Keep answers short, factual, and non-speculative. Pause, think, and answer what is asked.
- Don’t volunteer extra information or reinterpretations; if you don’t recall, say so.
- Avoid arguing with counsel or the judge; let your attorney object or intercede.
- Do not discuss the case outside privileged channels; avoid posts or messages about the case.
- Review the timeline and documents with Maria and do a mock Q&A to practice concise responses.
- Mark and organize exhibits exactly as counsel instructs; be ready to identify documents by date and source.
- If sensitive custody/financial info is involved, ask for protective orders and discuss how to present that evidence privately.

6) Questions to ask specifically about custody/financial intersections
- How might custody proceedings or existing custody orders affect this civil case or evidence admissibility?
- Could financial disclosures made in discovery affect custody or enforcement? How to protect that information?
- Do we need to involve family court counsel or coordinate with any existing family court orders?
- Are there special steps to limit use of sensitive financial/custody material (sealed filings, in camera review)?

7) Evidence preservation and discovery checklist
- Immediately preserve all documents and devices that may contain relevant info (emails, phones, cloud drives).
- Avoid deleting or altering files. Ask Maria about issuing a preservation letter.
- Confirm whether any forensic imaging or metadata preservation is needed.
- Prepare witness declarations and identify potential expert witnesses (for damages, forensic, custody economist).

8) Settlement and strategy questions
- Are we open to mediation or settlement prior to the preliminary hearing? What are realistic terms?
- What’s our bottom line and walk-away point?
- What settlement confidentiality protections should we require?
- If we pursue injunctive relief, what interim protections should we ask the court for?

9) Follow-up logistics to confirm with Maria
- Schedule a prep session (mock Q&A) before the hearing — request a date/time.
- Confirm what exhibits and declarations must be filed and when.
- Ask Maria to outline exactly which documents you should bring to court.
- Ask for a clear summary/email after your meeting listing next steps and deadlines.

10) How I can help next
- I can format these questions into a clean checklist or a brief email you can send to Maria Santos asking for a meeting and her expectations before the hearing. Would you like that?
- If you want, tell me whether you’re the plaintiff/petitioner or defendant/respondent and any key dates (hearing date), and I’ll tailor the checklist more precisely.

If you want the checklist converted into a short email to Maria or into a printable meeting agenda, tell me how formal you want it and I’ll draft it.

