Below is a practical, organized plan you can use to prepare for the preliminary hearing and possible trial, and a set of specific questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos). I’m not a lawyer — this is general preparation help. Run these items by Maria for legal strategy and any jurisdiction-specific advice for San Diego County, California.

1) Quick priorities to discuss with your attorney
- Confirm exactly what kind of hearing is coming up (case management conference / preliminary injunction / pretrial conference / motion hearing / initial hearing). The lawyer needs to tell you the purpose and what will be decided there.
- Ask whether you’ll be expected to testify at the hearing and whether there will be a live evidentiary record.
- Confirm discovery status and whether anything needs to be produced or filed before the hearing.
- Discuss confidentiality safeguards (protective order, sealing, redactions) for any custody or financial documents.

2) Specific questions to ask Maria Santos (grouped by topic)
Case overview & strategy
- What is our legal theory and the specific claims/defenses we will present for the NDA dispute?
- What outcome are we asking the court for at the preliminary hearing (scheduling, injunctive relief, preservation of evidence, etc.)?
- What is the likely timeline (next deadlines, trial window) and major milestones?

NDA-specific questions
- Exactly which provisions of the NDA are in dispute? Are there ambiguities we should expect the judge to focus on?
- What are the possible legal defenses the respondent will raise (e.g., public domain, prior knowledge, waiver, scope/overbreadth)?
- How do we prove the information was confidential and that you took steps to keep it confidential?

Evidence & discovery
- What documents or communications should I gather and provide immediately (signed NDA, emails/texts, notes, drafts, meeting logs)?
- Do we need forensic evidence (metadata, device logs)? If so, who arranges that and by when?
- What is our position on discovery requests — will we seek anything from the respondent (communications, financials, custody-related documents)?

Damages and remedies
- How do we quantify damages (actual loss, unjust enrichment, statutory or contractual remedies, injunctive relief)?
- What proof will we need to support damages or the need for an injunction?

Custody & financial matters (sensitive)
- How might financial records or custody issues be used against me, and how do we limit harm or disclosure?
- Should we move to seal or file sensitive documents under protective order? What’s the process in San Diego County?
- Will anything from the custody/financial situation affect credibility, and how should we address that?

Hearing logistics & procedure
- What will happen at the hearing step-by-step? (Who speaks first, evidence submission, time limits.)
- Do I need to bring witnesses or exhibits, and how should they be marked/submitted?
- Should I prepare a one-page facts/timeline summary for the judge and counsel?

Witnesses & testimony
- Who should testify for our case and what will each witness cover?
- Will I need coaching for direct and cross-examination? Can we run a mock cross?
- What are likely weak spots the respondent will attack, and how should I respond?

Privilege & communications
- Which communications are protected by attorney-client privilege or work product and should not be uploaded to shared systems?
- Is there any risk that communications with others (family, colleagues) could be used against me?

Settlement & costs
- What are the realistic settlement options and their pros/cons?
- What are expected costs to go to trial vs. settle? How do we handle cost-shifting if we win?

Pre-hearing checklist & deadlines
- What filings (briefs, exhibits, declarations) must be submitted in advance and when?
- What else must I do in the next 7–14 days to be hearing-ready?

3) Questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask you (so you can prepare short, factual answers)
Judge (procedural / case management)
- What relief are you seeking and why is it needed now?
- Is the case ready for trial/scheduling? What discovery remains?
- Have the parties discussed settlement or alternative dispute resolution?
- Are there urgent confidentiality or preservation concerns?

Opposing counsel (substantive / impeachment)
- When was the NDA signed, and what did you understand its scope to be?
- What specific information do you claim was disclosed in violation of the NDA? (be ready with dates and content)
- What steps did you take to protect the information (passwords, limited distribution, notices)?
- Were any disclosures authorized or made by someone else with permission?
- How did the respondent obtain the information — you will be asked to explain the chain of events clearly.
- What damages did you suffer and how are they calculated?
- Questions intended to undermine credibility: inconsistencies in your timeline, motives (financial pressure, custody disputes), prior disclosures of the same info.
- If custody/financial matters are relevant: why are those issues connected to the NDA claim? (Opposing counsel may try to paint motive or bias.)

4) How to prepare answers and handle questioning
- Be concise and factual. Practice answering in short declarative sentences.
- Do not volunteer extra information; answer only what’s asked.
- If you don’t recall, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing. Confirm details with Maria beforehand.
- Avoid arguing with opposing counsel or the judge; stay calm and ask to confer with Maria if necessary.
- Work with Maria to prepare a simple, chronological timeline of events you can refer to.

5) Documents and evidence checklist to bring/send to Maria now
- The executed NDA and any amendments
- All communications about the NDA (emails, texts, chat logs) with dates
- A clear timeline of events (dates, locations, participants)
- Copies of any allegedly disclosed materials and where they appeared
- Financial records that could be relevant (bank statements, payroll, contracts)
- Custody orders or filings that could be referenced
- Witness names and contact info, plus any written statements
- Device logs, backups, metadata, social posts relevant to disclosure
- Any prior settlement offers, demand letters, or correspondence with respondent
- Evidence of damages (invoices, lost opportunities, valuations)

6) Practical steps I recommend now
- Ask Maria for a pre-hearing call to run through likely questions and to do a mock cross-exam.
- Provide Maria the documents above in an organized folder (PDFs, native files with metadata when possible).
- Create a one-page “facts” summary and a separate timeline you can memorize.
- Ask Maria about protective orders or sealing procedures for custody/financial documents.
- Practice concise answers with Maria and agree on which topics to avoid or defer to counsel.

7) Want help drafting an email to Maria?
I can draft a concise email for you to send to Maria Santos that summarizes these questions and requests a meeting or mock preparation session. Would you like me to draft that now, and do you want to include the preliminary hearing date (if you have it) and the documents you’ve already collected?

If you give me the hearing date and whether you want a short or detailed email, I’ll draft it exactly as you can send.

