Good — I can help you get organized and make sure you and Maria Santos cover everything important before the hearing and, if needed, trial. Below are three parts you can use directly: (A) focused questions to ask your attorney, (B) likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask at the preliminary hearing and at trial (so you can prepare answers), and (C) practical preparation steps and documents to gather. I can also draft a short email or checklist you can send to Maria — tell me which you prefer.

A — Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case posture and timeline
  - What exactly will this upcoming preliminary hearing cover (case management conference, temporary orders, motion hearing, settlement conference)? Is “preliminary hearing” the correct term in our civil matter?
  - What are the next deadlines (discovery cutoff, motion cutoff, pretrial conference, trial date)?
  - What is our litigation strategy for the hearing and for trial if it proceeds?

- Claims, defenses, and burden
  - What are the legal elements the respondent must prove and what do we have to prove to establish breach of the NDA?
  - What defenses do we expect the respondent to assert (e.g., unenforceable NDA, overbroad, unconscionability, public policy, lack of consideration)?
  - What is the standard of proof for the relief we seek (damages, injunction, sanctions)?

- Evidence and witnesses
  - Which documents are essential to prove breach, damages, and intent (signed NDA, emails, text messages, drafts, payment records)?
  - Who are the witnesses we plan to call? Who might the respondent call?
  - Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., damages expert, IT forensics) and when must they be disclosed?

- Discovery and document protection
  - What outstanding discovery do we need and how will we get it (depositions, subpoenas, production requests)?
  - Do we need a protective order to keep sensitive documents (financials, custody records, personal information) confidential? If so, can you prepare a proposed protective order now?
  - How should we log or assert privilege (attorney-client, work product)? What should we do if privileged material was inadvertently disclosed?

- Financial and custody issues
  - How might the financial records or custody-related information affect the NDA claim or damages? What is relevant and what is likely to be excluded?
  - If custody or child-related information appears in discovery, how do we protect children’s privacy and prevent misuse of that material?
  - Could the respondent use financial/custody matters to attack credibility or motive? How should we respond?

- Motions and remedies
  - Should we seek a preliminary injunction or temporary restraining order to prevent further disclosures before trial?
  - Are we pursuing damages, injunctive relief, attorneys’ fees, sanctions? How do we prove each?
  - Are there any motions we should file before the hearing (motion to compel, protective order, motion in limine, demurrer, motion for summary judgment)?

- Witness and deposition preparation
  - Will you prepare me and other witnesses for deposition and court testimony? How long is prep and what should I practice saying?
  - Should I plan to testify at the preliminary hearing or at trial? What are the risks and benefits?

- Settlement and alternatives
  - What is a realistic settlement range given our facts and local practice in San Diego County?
  - Should we consider mediation before gearing up for trial? If so, when?
  - What are the costs of continuing to trial vs settling?

- Logistics and courtroom protocol
  - Where/when is the hearing, and will it be in-person or remote? What courtroom procedures and dress code should I follow?
  - How long will the hearing likely last and what should I expect when we go before the judge?

- Fees and costs
  - What are estimated attorney fees and costs through the hearing and through trial?
  - Are there fee-shifting provisions in the NDA that could make the respondent responsible for our fees if we prevail?

B — Questions the judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask (and how to be ready)
- Judge may ask at a case-management or preliminary hearing:
  - Are the parties ready for trial? (Be prepared with realistic timeline and trial length estimate.)
  - What are the outstanding discovery issues? (Have a short list of what’s needed and proposed deadlines.)
  - Have the parties conferred about settlement/mediation? (Be ready to state settlement posture.)
  - Are there any emergency or temporary relief requests (injunctions, sealing)? (Have grounds and evidence ready.)

- Opposing counsel likely will ask (written discovery, depositions, or cross-examination):
  - When and how did the alleged disclosure occur? Who saw it and where did it come from?
  - What specific provision of the NDA did you rely on — text, scope, duration? (Be able to cite clause and explain conduct breaching it.)
  - What damages or harm resulted from the disclosure? How are those damages quantified?
  - Did you take steps to mitigate harm after the disclosure? (Show actions you took.)
  - Who else had access to the information and could they have made the disclosure?
  - Were there any communications that modified or waived the NDA? (Be prepared about any side agreements or oral modifications.)
  - Why was the information confidential or proprietary? (Explain business or personal stakes.)
  - If custody/financial matters arise: why are those records relevant? How do they relate to the NDA claim or damages?

- Questions you personally may be asked on the stand:
  - Did you sign the NDA? When and under what circumstances?
  - Did you understand the NDA’s terms? Who explained it to you?
  - Describe exactly what was disclosed and when you first learned of it.
  - What efforts did you make to contain the disclosure?
  - What are the specific financial losses that resulted?
  - Any inconsistencies in prior statements or documents will be challenged — be prepared to explain dates, actions, and memory limitations.

How to answer (general guidance to practice with your lawyer):
- Short, truthful, and direct answers. Don’t volunteer extra details.
- If you don’t remember, say so — don’t guess.
- Ask your attorney if you should invoke privilege or exercise the right not to answer a question in certain settings.
- Always consult Maria before giving substantive written statements outside of discovery response requirements.

C — Practical checklist: documents, witness list, and prep actions
- Documents to assemble (with dates and brief index):
  - Signed NDA (all versions), any amendments, emails negotiating the NDA.
  - All communications (emails, texts, DMs) referencing the confidential information and any alleged disclosures.
  - Copies/screenshots of the disclosed material (if accessible) and where it was published/shared.
  - Financial records showing damages or links to damages (bank records, contracts, invoices, lost business proofs).
  - Any correspondence about mitigation steps (cease-and-desist, takedown notices).
  - Custody or family court documents only if relevant — keep them organized and discuss confidentiality with your attorney.
  - Logs of who had access to the information and timeline (timeline document is very helpful).

- Witnesses to identify and prepare:
  - Yourself and anyone who executed or negotiated the NDA.
  - Any employees/colleagues who handled the confidential info.
  - Recipients of the disclosed information and those who can testify about its dissemination.
  - Experts (damages, IT forensics, reputation damages).
  - Custody/child-related witnesses only if directly relevant — discuss protective measures.

- Procedural actions to request now:
  - Preservation letter to the respondent to preserve relevant evidence (spoliation prevention).
  - Draft a proposed protective order covering sensitive financial or custody materials.
  - Prepare a privilege log for any withheld documents.
  - Consider a motion for emergency relief (TRO/preliminary injunction) if ongoing harm is likely.

- Witness prep checklist:
  - Review timeline and documents chronologically.
  - Practice direct-examination answers; practice short, clear, consistent responses.
  - Review possible cross-examination lines (credibility, motive, inconsistencies).
  - Go over courtroom demeanor and rules about addressing the judge.

Other practical items
- Ask Maria whether she will: handle filings, set up subpoenas, coordinate experts, and represent you at the hearing.
- Confirm how and when she will keep you updated and how to reach her during the hearing if needed.
- Bring hard copies of exhibits, exhibit list, and a concise binder with timeline and key documents to the hearing.

Next steps I can do for you
- Draft a concise email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and asking for specific confirmations (hearing type, documents requested, protective order). OR
- Create a printable one-page checklist you can bring to the hearing and to prep sessions.

Which would you like me to prepare next?

