Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a good place to start. Below I’ve organized (1) the kinds of questions the judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask you in a nondisclosure agreement (NDA) dispute (including areas where financial or custody facts could be raised), (2) exact questions you can ask your attorney Maria Santos to prepare, and (3) practical documents and prep steps to bring to the hearing and trial prep.

If you want, I can draft an email to Maria Santos with these questions or role‑play the Q&A so you can practice.

A. What the judge or opposing counsel may ask you — likely topics and example questions
- The NDA itself and its scope
  - “When did you sign the NDA and who presented it?”
  - “What information was covered by the NDA? Can you identify it specifically?”
  - “Did you understand the terms when you signed it? Were you represented/before signing?”
  - “Has the NDA been modified, renewed or terminated in writing or by conduct?”

- Alleged breach and your conduct
  - “What exactly did you disclose or do that they say violated the NDA?”
  - “When, where, and to whom did any disclosure occur?”
  - “Why did you disclose the information? Was it accidental or intentional?”
  - “Did you receive authorization or a waiver to disclose?”

- Harm / damages and mitigation
  - “What harm resulted from the alleged disclosure?”
  - “How did you respond when you learned about the alleged breach?”
  - “What steps did you take to mitigate the harm (recalls, retractions, notices)?”

- Intent and credibility
  - “Were you aware the information was confidential?”
  - “Have you previously disclosed similar information elsewhere?”
  - “Do you have any reason to gain financially or otherwise from the disclosure?”

- Evidence authenticity and chain of custody
  - “How did you obtain these documents/emails/messages?”
  - “Can you confirm these are unaltered copies and explain how they were produced?”

- Privilege, confidentiality, and protected communications
  - “Were any communications with your attorney or involving privileged matters?”
  - “Do you contend any materials are privileged or protected from disclosure?”

- Financial issues (relevance to damages/fees)
  - “What financial losses did you or the other party suffer?”
  - “Do you have documentation (bank statements, invoices) showing financial impact?”
  - “Are you seeking attorneys’ fees or sanctions? What’s your ability to pay or recover?”

- Custody/child/family matters (how these might surface)
  - Opposing counsel or judge may raise custody/household finances only if relevant to credibility, financial status, or motive: e.g., ability to pay sanctions/fees or motive to disclose for financial gain or custody leverage.
  - Possible questions: “Has your family or custody situation affected your decisions about disclosure?” “Are any communications with caregivers or custodial exchanges relevant?”

- Procedural / jurisdictional and remedy questions
  - “What remedy are you seeking? Injunction, damages, declaratory relief?”
  - “Have you sought a protective order for discovery?”
  - “Are you asking the court for expedited relief (TRO/preliminary injunction)?”

B. Exact questions to ask Maria Santos (use these in your appointment)
- Clarify the hearing type and immediate procedure
  - “Is the upcoming hearing a case management conference, preliminary injunction hearing, or something else? What will happen at this hearing?”
  - “What is the judge likely to decide at this hearing versus at trial?”

- Evidence & documents
  - “Which documents and communications should I bring today/for the hearing?”
  - “Do you want original devices (phones, laptops) or copies? How should they be organized?”
  - “Which documents do we need to authenticate or get affidavits for?”

- Anticipated lines of questioning
  - “What questions do you expect the opposing counsel to ask me at the hearing? The judge?”
  - “About which facts do you think I will be cross‑examined most heavily?”

- Custody/financial relevance
  - “How could my custody situation or family finances be used by opposing counsel? What should I disclose or avoid saying?”
  - “Will my financial documents be required or could they be sealed/protected?”

- Privilege and confidentiality
  - “Which communications are privileged and how do we assert privilege without waiving it?”
  - “Should we file a motion for protective order or for in‑camera review of sensitive documents?”

- Strategy & remedies
  - “Do you recommend pursuing a preliminary injunction, a settlement, or taking this to trial?”
  - “What are realistic outcomes and timing if we go to trial in San Diego County civil division?”

- Preparation & witness support
  - “Who should we list as witnesses and what statements will they need to prepare?”
  - “Can we do a mock direct/cross examination prep session? How long will that take?”

- Discovery and motions
  - “What discovery do we need immediately (emails, phone records, metadata) and what should we subpoena?”
  - “Any motions we should file now (motion to compel, motion for sanctions, motion in limine)?”

- Costs, timeline, & settlement posture
  - “What are estimated costs to proceed to trial vs settlement?”
  - “What is your recommended settlement range and strategy? When should we attempt to negotiate?”

- Courtroom logistics & etiquette
  - “How should I present myself and answer questions in court? Any phrases to avoid?”
  - “Will I be required to testify at the preliminary hearing or only at trial?”

- Administrative
  - “Who will be our lead contact at Santos Law Office on daily matters?”
  - “Will you request sealing orders for any filings that include sensitive personal/custody info?”

C. Documents and prep you should bring / compile
- The NDA(s): signed copies, any amendments, cover emails or drafts showing negotiation.
- All communications: emails, text messages, social media messages, Voicemails, notes referencing the confidential info — include metadata if possible (dates/times).
- Evidence of disclosure: copies of what was disclosed (if any), who received it, when and where (screenshots with timestamps).
- Evidence of harm: contracts lost, invoices, screenshots, financial statements, witness statements.
- Financial documents: recent pay stubs, bank statements, tax returns (relevant to damages or ability to pay).
- Custody-related documents only if relevant: custody orders, court filings, child support statements — discuss with Maria before disclosing widely.
- Device and forensic info: chain of custody for devices, forensic reports if available.
- Witness list and short summary of what each witness will say.
- Draft chronology/timeline of events — concise single‑page chronology is helpful.

D. Litigation and evidentiary issues to confirm with Maria
- Ask whether any evidence needs forensic verification (metadata, hash values).
- Whether to move to seal or redact any filing that reveals private or custody information.
- How to assert privilege (what to produce vs withhold) and how to log withheld documents via a privilege log.
- Whether to ask the court for a protective order to limit discovery or public disclosure.
- Which factual admissions are safe to make and which points to contest vigorously.
- Whether settlement discussions should be facilitated through mediation and when to propose it.

E. Practical trial/hearing prep and communication tips
- Keep answers short and factual; avoid volunteering extra details or speculating.
- If you don’t remember an exact date/time, say so and offer to provide documents rather than guessing.
- Avoid discussing custody issues in open court unless your attorney says it’s necessary and protected.
- Practice concise, plain-language explanations of what happened and why (2–3 sentence versions).
- Ask Maria for a short “script” of how to introduce yourself and summarize your position under oath.

F. Quick checklist to bring to your meeting with Maria Santos
- One‑page factual timeline
- Copies of the NDA and key communications
- List of potential witnesses and contact info
- Any forensic or device evidence
- Questions you want answered from section B (bring this list)

Closing / next steps
- I can draft a short email to Maria Santos with the above questions and ask for any pre‑hearing tasks (documents to bring, mock prep). Would you like me to draft that email now?
- Or, if you prefer, I can role‑play likely opposing counsel/judge questions so you can practice answers. Which would you like next?

