Good — I can help you get organized and ready. Below are focused things to ask your attorney (Maria Santos) plus the types of questions a judge or opposing counsel might ask you at a preliminary hearing or at trial, with short notes on how to prepare to answer them. Use this as a checklist you can take to your next meeting.

A. Priority questions to ask your attorney (what to get from Maria)
- Case status & timeline
  - What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing in the civil division (purpose, procedure, likely length)?
  - What are the next court deadlines and critical dates (motions, discovery cutoffs, pretrial conference, trial date)?
  - What’s our preferred timeline and strategy (settlement vs. motion practice vs. trial)?

- Legal theory & exposure
  - What causes of action will we assert (breach of NDA, injunctive relief, damages, etc.) and what must we prove for each?
  - What defenses can the respondent raise? Which are likely to succeed?
  - Burdens of proof and legal standards that apply in San Diego civil court for injunctive relief and damages.

- Evidence & admissibility
  - What documents, emails, texts, recordings, or witnesses do we need to prove breach and damages?
  - How should we preserve evidence (metadata, originals)? Any forensic steps we should take now?
  - What evidence is likely to be excluded and why (hearsay, privileged communication)?
  - Should we seek a protective order or sealing order for sensitive/financial/custody information?

- Discovery & witness preparation
  - What discovery should we serve (interrogatories, document requests, depositions)? What will we likely receive?
  - Do we need subpoenas for third parties (email providers, banks, employers)?
  - Which witnesses should be deposed and which should testify? How will you prepare witnesses?
  - Do we need any experts (forensic IT, financial damages, custody expert)?

- Damages & remedies
  - What types of damages or remedies can we ask for (compensatory, consequential, punitive, attorneys’ fees, injunction)?
  - How will we calculate damages and what proof will be required?
  - What are reasonable settlement parameters (range, terms, confidentiality)?

- Custody & financial issues
  - How might custody or financial details be used by the respondent or the court? What is legally relevant vs. what’s prejudicial?
  - If family/custody matters overlap, will those issues be heard in this civil case or separately? How to coordinate?
  - How to protect sensitive financial/custody documents from public filing (redaction, sealing motions)?

- Courtroom logistics & client conduct
  - Will I need to testify at the preliminary hearing? If so, when and in what format?
  - If the judge or opposing counsel questions me directly, what should I do (short answers, consult attorney, avoid volunteering)?
  - Local rules for San Diego County civil division: exhibit numbering, filing format, in-court procedures, filing deadlines — confirm specifics.
  - Practicals: where to arrive, security, dress, electronic exhibits, length of hearing, whether remote appearance is allowed.

- Motions & pretrial strategy
  - Should we file any immediate motions (temporary injunctive relief, motion to compel discovery, protective order, motion in limine)?
  - Are there grounds for sanctions or to seek expedited discovery?

- Fees & costs
  - Likely cost estimate to get to hearing and to trial; payment arrangements; whether fee-shifting is possible under the NDA.
  - Options to limit costs (targeted discovery, mediation).

B. Questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask you (and how to prepare)
Note: For each, prepare concise, factual answers and practice them with Maria. Don’t guess; if you don’t know, say so and offer to provide the document/statement later.

Common questions about the NDA and the alleged breach
- When did you sign the NDA? Who signed it on behalf of the other party?
  - Prep: have the signed NDA with dates and signatory names ready.
- What is the specific provision you say was breached? Show the clause and explain the factual basis.
  - Prep: mark up the NDA and prepare a one-sentence summary of the breach.
- When did you first become aware of the alleged breach? What exactly did you learn and from whom?
  - Prep: timeline with dates, sources, and supporting documents.
- What communications (emails, messages, calls) show the respondent violated the NDA?
  - Prep: gather originals, printouts, and metadata; label exhibits.
- Did you give the respondent notice and an opportunity to cure (if required by the NDA)?
  - Prep: copies of cease-and-desist, demand letters, or other notices with dates.

Questions on damages and remedies
- What harm did you suffer as a result of the breach (financial loss, lost business, reputational harm)?
  - Prep: documentation of financial loss, invoices, contracts, valuation methods.
- Are you seeking an injunction? Why is injunctive relief necessary and urgent?
  - Prep: demonstrate irreparable harm and inadequate remedy at law if seeking injunction.

Questions on defenses, scope, and enforceability
- How is the NDA limited in scope (time, geography, subject matter)? Is it overbroad or ambiguous?
  - Prep: be ready to explain intended scope and business purpose.
- Has the respondent raised any counterarguments (consent, public interest, information already public)?
  - Prep: anticipate these defenses and have evidence to counter them.

Questions about custody/financial issues (if they become relevant)
- Why are custody or personal financial details in evidence or relevant to this case?
  - Prep: work with Maria to determine relevance and prepare justifications or motions to exclude.
- Have you disclosed these details to anyone? Could disclosure harm minors or other third parties?
  - Prep: identify sensitive items and propose protective measures.

Cross-examination style questions you may be asked
- Can you walk me through, step by step, what happened on [specific date]?
  - Prep: rehearsed timeline, simple factual statements.
- Did you ever authorize the disclosure/use of this information?
  - Prep: be factual—if you did, explain circumstances; if not, say no.
- Have you kept copies of all communications? Have any documents been deleted?
  - Prep: confirm preservation actions; if something is missing, be honest and explain.
- Why did you wait X days/months to bring this to court?
  - Prep: explain reasons: investigation, attempts to resolve, counsel discussions.

C. Evidence & document checklist to bring to your meeting and to court
- Original signed NDA and any amendments
- All communications with the respondent (email chains, texts, DMs, letters) in chronological order
- Demand/cease-and-desist letters and your responses
- Timeline of events with dates and short descriptions
- Financial records showing claimed damages (contracts, invoices, bank statements)
- Any public statements, social media posts, or third-party disclosures relevant to the alleged breach
- Witness list and short summaries of what each witness will say
- Any expert reports or evaluations (IT forensics, financial expert)
- Notes of any attempts to resolve or mediate

D. Procedural/legal protections and practical tips to ask Maria to do
- File a preservation/hold notice and forensic imaging if electronic evidence is at risk
- Move for protective order or sealing if financial/custody evidence is sensitive
- Consider a narrowly tailored temporary restraining order or preliminary injunction if immediate action is needed
- Prepare for a deposition: coordinate with counsel for mock deposition practice
- If testimony might touch on custody/financial matters, ask about limiting scope and pretrial motions to exclude prejudicial information

E. How to prepare personally for questioning
- Keep answers short and factual; don’t volunteer extra information
- If you don’t remember a fact, say “I don’t recall” rather than guess
- Avoid arguing with opposing counsel in court; let your attorney object and handle disputes
- Rehearse your timeline and keep it consistent with documents
- Don’t communicate with the respondent directly — let your attorney handle it

F. Suggested short list you can hand Maria at your next meeting
- “Please explain the court’s likely questions at the preliminary hearing and whether I will be called to speak.”
- “Which documents should I produce immediately and which should be protected/sealed?”
- “What defenses should I expect and how do we rebut them?”
- “Should I expect to testify at the preliminary hearing or only at trial?”
- “What settlement parameters would you recommend given our position and likely costs?”
- “Can we run a mock Q&A so I can practice concise answers?”

G. Next steps I recommend you take now
- Send everything relevant (even awkward or damaging items) to Maria so she can evaluate the full record.
- Create a clear dated timeline (one page) of events for counsel and one detailed folder with originals.
- Ask Maria about immediate preservation steps for electronic evidence.
- Schedule a prep session for deposition/testimony practice.

If you want, I can:
- Turn this into a printable one-page checklist for your meeting with Maria.
- Draft a short email to Maria listing these questions and asking for a meeting and mock-prep session.
- Create a sample timeline template you can fill out.

