  - What defenses or counterarguments can the respondent raise (e.g., unconscionability, overbroad terms, lack of consideration, public policy)?
  - How strong is our evidence on breach and on damages or injunctive relief?
- Evidence and proof
  - What specific facts and documents do we need to establish breach, harm, and remedies?
  - What evidence will the respondent likely try to use against me, and how can we counter it?
  - Are any documents or communications privileged? How should we handle those?
- Witnesses and experts
  - Who should we call as witnesses? Do we need expert testimony (forensics, valuation, childcare/psychologist if custody issues are relevant)?
  - Do you recommend I testify? What are the pros and cons?
- Discovery and preservation
  - What discovery requests should we serve (interrogatories, requests for production, subpoenas)?
  - What immediate steps should I take to preserve evidence (litigation hold, save emails, back up devices)?
- Motions and remedies
  - Will we seek a preliminary injunction or temporary restraining order? If so, what are the standards and what evidence do we need now?
  - What damages or equitable relief are we seeking (monetary damages, injunctive relief, attorneys’ fees)?
- Custody and financial issues
  - Could custody or the other party’s financial situation influence damages, remedies, or credibility? If so, how should we incorporate or shield those issues?
  - Should we coordinate with family-law counsel if custody is or will be litigated separately?
- Settlement and negotiation
  - What is our position on settlement now? What settlement terms are acceptable or unacceptable?
  - Do you recommend mediation or a settlement conference before trial?
- Practicalities and client conduct
  - How should I respond to requests or communications from opposing counsel going forward?
  - What social media or public comments should I avoid?
  - How should I document and report new developments to you?
- Costs and risk management
  - What are projected attorney fees, court costs, and other expenses through the preliminary hearing and through trial?
  - What are the realistic outcomes and odds of success?
- Logistics for hearing/trial
  - What do I need to bring to the hearing (exhibits, witness list, pre-marked exhibits, copies for court and opposing counsel)?
  - Will there be remote appearances or is it in-person at San Diego County Civil Division? What courtroom procedures should I know?

B. Questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask you
(Practice short, honest answers; don’t volunteer extra information.)

- Background and relationship to the NDA
  - Who drafted the NDA and when was it signed?
  - Did you fully understand the terms when you signed? Were there any negotiations or revisions?
  - What role did you have (employee, consultant, contractor, investor)?
- Facts about the alleged disclosures/breach
  - Precisely what information was disclosed, when, how, and to whom?
  - Were disclosures intentional, negligent, or accidental?
  - Were there any prior permissions, consents, or exceptions that apply?
- Knowledge and intent
  - Did you know the information was confidential as defined by the NDA?
  - Why did the disclosure occur? (Be factual—opposing counsel will probe for motive.)
- Efforts to mitigate and prevent harm
  - What steps did you take after learning of the disclosure (notify counsel, notify the other party, attempt to contain or retrieve information)?
  - Did you attempt to mitigate damages?
- Damages and remedies
  - What specific harm resulted from the breach (financial losses, lost business, reputational harm)?
  - How did you calculate or document damages?
- Custody and financial questions (if relevant)
  - Is there any pending family/custody litigation that overlaps or affects this case?
  - Are there financial pressures or motives the judge/opposing counsel might use to challenge your credibility (e.g., debts, financial disputes)?
- Credibility and consistency
  - Have you made prior statements (emails, texts, social posts) that differ from your current testimony? Be prepared to explain.
  - Were any documents or messages deleted or altered? If so, why?
- Procedural / readiness questions (likely from judge)
  - Are counsel and parties ready to proceed as scheduled?
  - What is the status of discovery and outstanding motions?
  - Is there any need for a protective order or sealing?

C. Documents and evidence to prepare and bring to the meeting/hearing
- Contract and related documents
  - Original signed NDA and any drafts, amendments, or related agreements
  - Any documents showing negotiation or acceptance (emails, tracked changes)
- Communications
  - All emails, text messages, chat logs, and call logs relevant to the NDA and alleged disclosure
  - Messages that show intent, knowledge, permission, or lack thereof
- Evidence of disclosure
  - Copies/screenshots of the disclosed materials as they appeared to recipients
  - Dates/times and recipient identities for every alleged disclosure
  - Metadata, file histories, or digital forensics if available
- Evidence of harm/damages
  - Financial records (invoices, bank statements, lost contracts, valuation reports)
  - Business records showing lost opportunities or revenue
  - Expert reports (if already obtained) or contact info for potential experts
- Custody/financial documents (if relevant)
  - Any court orders, custody agreements, or financial declarations that intersect with the dispute
- Preservation proof
  - Evidence that you preserved devices, backed up data, and issued a litigation hold
- Witness info
  - Names and contact information of witnesses and a short summary of expected testimony
- Court filings and procedural documents
  - Complaints, answers, motions, declarations, prior hearing orders, discovery responses
- Exhibit list and copies
  - Pre-marked exhibits with copies for the court and opposing counsel (follow local rules)

D. Testimony and courtroom tips
- Answering questions
  - Pause before you speak; don’t rush.
  - Listen to the whole question. If you don’t understand, say “Could you rephrase that?” or “I don’t understand the question.”
  - Give short, direct answers. Don’t volunteer extra information.
  - Always tell the truth. If you don’t remember, say “I don’t remember” instead of guessing.
- On cross-examination
  - Expect the opposing counsel to test your credibility and motive. Stay calm and avoid arguing.
  - If confronted with a document, answer about your knowledge of it honestly and briefly.
- Communication with your lawyer
  - Signal to your lawyer if you need a break, or if an objection should be made.
- Practicalities
  - Dress professionally, arrive early, bring hard copies of exhibits and notes.
  - Turn off phones; keep private notes out of sight unless instructed by counsel.

E. Immediate next steps you can take now
- Ask Maria the procedural clarifying questions above at your next meeting (or send them this checklist).
- Issue a litigation hold immediately if you haven’t already (preserve emails, phones, cloud accounts, devices).
- Gather and organize the most important documents now: NDA, all communications about the NDA, proof of disclosure, and any evidence of harm.
- Prepare a chronological timeline of events (dates, people, documents) you can review with Maria.
- Decide whether you will testify and discuss mock examination practice with Maria.
- Consider whether a mediator or settlement discussion is appropriate before the preliminary hearing.

Would you like:
- A printable checklist version of this list?
- A draft email to Maria Santos with these questions and requests for a meeting?
- Help practicing answers to likely questions with mock Q&A?

Also — quick clarifying questions for more tailored suggestions:
1) Is the upcoming hearing a preliminary injunction/TRO hearing, a case management conference, or another kind of preliminary hearing?
2) Are you expected to testify at that hearing?
3) Is the custody matter in the same case or a separate family-court matter?

Answering those will let me tailor the likely judge questions and document checklist more precisely.

