Below are suggested questions to ask your attorney and a set of likely questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask (with short points you should be ready to answer). Treat these as preparation prompts — confirm specifics and strategy with Maria Santos before responding in court or to opposing counsel.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & next steps
  - What exactly will be decided at the upcoming hearing? (TRO/preliminary injunction, case management, discovery disputes, etc.)
  - What is the timeline to trial and key upcoming deadlines?
  - Are there any immediate deadlines I need to meet (discovery responses, document production, motions)?

- Claims and defenses
  - What specific legal claims are we making (breach of NDA, misappropriation of trade secrets, declaratory relief, injunctive relief, damages)?
  - What defenses or counterclaims might the respondent raise?
  - How strong is our legal position under California law? Any legal risks I should know about?

- Evidence and documents
  - Exactly which documents should I bring to the hearing and to trial? (signed NDA, email chains, files, logs, versions, communications, financial/custody records that are relevant)
  - Do we have an unbroken chain of custody/metadata for electronic files showing dates/authors?
  - Should we prepare a chronology/timeline of events? Can you help draft it?

- Witnesses and testimony
  - Which witnesses will we call (fact witnesses, experts)? What will each witness say?
  - Do any witnesses need subpoenas or declarations for the hearing?
  - Should I expect to be cross-examined? How should I prepare?

- Discovery & motions
  - What discovery is outstanding? Do we need interrogatories, requests for production, depositions?
  - Are we planning any immediate motions (motion for preliminary injunction, motion to compel, motion for protective order, motion to seal)?
  - Are we seeking a protective order for sensitive documents? What form will it take?

- Remedies & strategy
  - What remedies are we asking for (injunction, damages, accounting, attorneys’ fees)?
  - What is our fallback plan if the judge denies injunctive relief?
  - Is settlement likely? What are settlement parameters you recommend?

- Costs, timeline & risks
  - What are the likely costs if we proceed to trial? What are probable timelines and risks?
  - Are there statute-of-limitations or other deadline concerns (e.g., for written contract claims in CA)?

- Confidentiality & custodial/financial issues
  - How will custody or financial records be used against me? What can we do to limit disclosure?
  - Are there special steps for keeping sensitive financial/custody materials out of public court filings (requests to seal, redaction, in-camera review)?
  - How do we protect privileged communications while producing documents?

- Practical courtroom prep
  - How should I answer questions in court (concise, stick to facts, avoid volunteering information)?
  - Do we need a mock examination or practice session before hearing/trial?
  - Who will be present at the hearing from your office?

- Communication and next steps
  - How and when will you update me about developments?
  - Can you draft a short list of items you need from me before the hearing?

2) Questions the judge might ask at a preliminary hearing or injunction hearing (be ready with concise answers)
- Procedural/standing
  - What relief are you asking the court to grant today? (e.g., temporary restraining order, preliminary injunction, discovery relief)
  - Why do you have standing to bring this claim?

- Likelihood of success and harm
  - What is the basis for saying you’re likely to succeed on the merits of the NDA claim?
  - What irreparable harm will occur if the respondent is not enjoined immediately?
  - What efforts did you make to mitigate harm (e.g., demand letters, cease-and-desist)?

- Evidence and scope
  - Can you identify the exact provisions of the NDA that were breached?
  - Which specific documents or information were disclosed or misused?
  - Who had access, and how was the information disclosed?

- Balance of equities & public interest
  - How will an injunction affect the respondent or third parties?
  - Why does the balance of hardships favor you?
  - Is there any public interest implicated?

- Practicalities and enforcement
  - How would the injunction be worded and enforced?
  - Do you want an order preserving potentially privileged or sensitive documents pending review?

3) Questions opposing counsel might ask (direct questioning, deposition or cross-exam)
- Authorship & signature
  - Did you sign this NDA? When and under what circumstances?
  - Who negotiated or modified the NDA? Were any versions emailed or redlined?

- Knowledge and disclosure
  - What exactly did you disclose and to whom?
  - Were any disclosures authorized or permitted under the NDA exceptions?
  - Did you reasonably treat the information as confidential?

- Intent & damages
  - Was there any intent to harm the respondent or third parties?
  - What actual damages or losses did you suffer because of the alleged breach?
  - Did you take steps to protect the information (passwords, access logs, NDAs with third parties)?

- Credibility & consistency
  - Are the dates in your timeline accurate? Why does this document differ from that email?
  - Have you ever made contradictory statements about the events?
  - Why did you wait to bring this suit (if there was a delay)?

- Custody/financial angles (if relevant)
  - How are your custody or financial circumstances relevant to this case?
  - Are you seeking this relief for a financial motive? (Be prepared to explain relevance calmly)
  - Have custody or financial disputes affected access to the allegedly confidential materials?

4) How to prepare responses — practical tips
- Keep answers short and factual: state facts, dates, documents, and avoid speculation or providing extra detail not asked for.
- Use a clear timeline: prepare a one-page chronology with dates, people involved, and documents for each event.
- Highlight key documents: identify the single most compelling document(s) that prove breach or harm (signed NDA, email showing disclosure, proof of misuse).
- Gather metadata where possible: file timestamps, access logs, version history.
- Prepare witness summaries: one-paragraph summary of what each witness will say and why they're relevant.
- Practice: rehearse responses with your attorney; do a mock deposition or hearing to simulate cross-examination.

5) Documents and evidence checklist to bring to your attorney and to court
- Copy of the signed NDA(s) and any prior versions or related agreements.
- All communications (emails, texts, chat logs) referencing the NDA, disclosures, or confidential material.
- Files/attachments alleged to have been disclosed (with metadata if available).
- Financial records or custody documents that may be used in the case (clearly labeled and explained why relevant).
- Any demand letters, cease-and-desist communications, and responses.
- Witness contact information and short bios.
- A prepared timeline/chronology and a list of exhibits with brief descriptions.

6) Custody and financial details — specific questions to raise with Maria
- How will custody/financial records be introduced or used? Are they material to the NDA claim or only collateral?
- Can we limit disclosure of sensitive custody files by redaction, in-camera review, or sealing?
- If financial issues create additional leverage or vulnerabilities, how should we handle them strategically?
- Does disclosure of financial/custody info affect claims for damages or equitable relief?

7) Procedure & local rules (San Diego County)
- Ask Maria to confirm any local rules or judge-specific practices for filing and for sealing/confidentiality in San Diego County civil division.
- Confirm timeline for obtaining protective orders or expedited discovery if needed.

8) Practical conduct tips for hearings and depositions
- Never speak to opposing counsel without your attorney present (or follow attorney guidance).
- In hearings: answer the judge directly, briefly, and honestly. If you don’t know, say so and offer to follow up.
- For depositions: pause, think, and consult with counsel if uncertain — be truthful, don’t volunteer extra facts.

9) Sample short answers you can practice
- “Yes, I signed the NDA on [date].”
- “I shared document X with person Y on [date]; I believed it was confidential and not authorized to be shared.”
- “We attempted to resolve this by sending a cease-and-desist on [date]; there was no satisfactory response.”
- “I am seeking injunctive relief to prevent further unauthorized use and damages for the losses identified in our exhibit list.”

10) Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise “questions for counsel” email you can send to Maria.
- Create a one-page timeline/chronology from details you provide.
- Build a checklist of documents to collect and produce.
- Role-play likely hearing or deposition questions (mock Q&A).

Which of the above would you like me to prepare for you now? If you want the email to Maria, tell me whether you want it formal or informal and any specific deadlines or hearing dates to include.

