I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Before I list questions and prep steps, one quick clarification: when you say “preliminary hearing,” do you mean a preliminary injunction / temporary restraining order hearing, a case-management conference, or something else? Civil cases don’t always use “preliminary hearing,” so I want to make sure the suggestions match the actual event.

Below are focused questions to take to Maria Santos and concrete things to prepare. I’ve grouped them so you can use or copy them into notes or an email.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- What type of hearing is this exactly (TRO/preliminary injunction, case management, status, motion hearing)? What will the judge expect at this stage?
- What are the specific legal elements we must prove for the claim (and any counterclaims) — i.e., what does the court need to see to rule in our favor?
- If this is an injunction request: what evidence will satisfy irreparable harm, likelihood of success, balance of equities, and public interest?
- What questions is the judge most likely to ask me about the facts, evidence, and requested relief?
- What lines of questioning should I expect from opposing counsel at the hearing or in depositions?
- What documents and witnesses do we need to bring or make available at the hearing?
- Do we need an expert (e.g., damages expert, forensic expert) and, if so, when should we retain them?
- How should I answer questions about custody and financial matters so they help — not harm — our position? Are there particular disclosures we must make?
- Are there privilege concerns (attorney-client, work product) with any documents or communications we have? How do we handle them (privilege log, redaction, motion to seal)?
- Should we ask the court for a protective order or to seal any filings because of confidentiality/NDA issues?
- What are the likely procedural next steps after this hearing? (deadlines, discovery schedule, motion practice, trial date estimates)
- What discovery will the respondent likely request? What discovery should we proactively seek?
- What is our settlement posture and what settlement options should I consider? Are there confidentiality or parenting/custody implications in settlement terms?
- What is the estimated cost / timeline to take this to trial versus settle?
- Do any local San Diego County rules or judges’ procedures affect how we should present confidential materials or conduct the hearing?
- How should I conduct myself during direct exam and cross-exam? What phrases/answers should I avoid?
- Can we run a mock Q&A or deposition practice session? How much time do you recommend for preparation?

Questions the judge is likely to ask (by hearing type)
- Preliminary injunction/TRO:
  - What specific relief are you requesting?
  - What are the facts showing irreparable harm (and why monetary damages aren’t adequate)?
  - Why is there a likelihood of success on the merits?
  - What is the scope and duration of the injunction you want?
  - Should a bond be required, and if so, how much?
- Case-management / scheduling:
  - What are the disputed issues?
  - Proposed schedule for discovery, expert disclosures, dispositive motions, and trial.
  - Are there motions or discovery disputes needing resolution before trial?
- Motions in limine / evidentiary hearings:
  - Is the evidence you seek to admit relevant and authenticated?
  - Are there confidentiality concerns that require sealing or redaction?

Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask (depositions, hearings, trial)
- Factual foundation:
  - Did you sign the NDA? When and under what circumstances?
  - Were you given time to review/obtain counsel? Was there consideration for the agreement?
  - What exactly was disclosed, to whom, and when?
  - Did you share the information publicly or with third parties?
- Intent and damages:
  - What motive did you have for disclosing or withholding information?
  - What are your actual damages and how were they calculated?
  - What steps did you take to mitigate damages?
- Authenticity and timeline:
  - Can you verify the documents, emails, and metadata? Who created them and when?
  - Are there conflicting statements you made earlier (social media, prior emails)?
- Custody/financial lines of attack:
  - How do custody arrangements or financial circumstances relate to this dispute?
  - Are you seeking tactical advantage in this legal dispute to influence custody or finances?
- Impeachment lines:
  - Questions to expose inconsistencies or memory lapses; they may try to get you to overstate or guess.

Documents and evidence to gather now
- The executed NDA (all versions, dated).
- All communications referencing the NDA or the allegedly disclosed information: emails, texts, messaging apps, letters.
- Any drafts or revisions of the NDA and evidence of negotiation (shows assent/consideration).
- Logs or evidence showing who accessed the confidential material (access logs, share links, downloads).
- Records showing damages or financial impact (invoices, lost contracts, financial statements).
- Custody/financial documents you’re worried might be relevant (custody orders, income statements) — only share with counsel as needed.
- Witness names and contact info (who saw the disclosure, who can attest to confidentiality).
- Metadata/backups/forensic copies of devices if digital evidence is central.
- Any prior consent, waivers, or public disclosures that undermine confidentiality.
- Documents showing mitigation efforts (cease-and-desist letters, steps to prevent further disclosure).

Practical trial/hearing preparation tips
- Create a timeline of events with dates and supporting documents; keep it concise (1–2 pages).
- Index exhibits and keep originals plus clean copies; have Bates numbers and exhibit list ready.
- Do a mock deposition/hearing with your attorney; practice concise, truthful answers and avoiding speculation.
- On cross-exam: short answers, never volunteer extra information, ask to confer with counsel if a question implicates privilege.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing. If you need to check a document, ask for a moment.
- Preserve evidence — do not delete messages or dispose of devices. Ask counsel about a litigation hold.
- Discuss whether to seek a protective order (to limit disclosure of documents) or to file sealed exhibits for particularly sensitive materials.
- Confirm courtroom logistics (where to sit, how exhibits are presented, whether electronic filing/paper copies are required) — local rules differ by San Diego courthouse.

Questions about discovery, motions, and strategy to raise with Maria
- Should we move for a preliminary injunction or wait for more discovery?
- Should we file any pre-hearing motions (e.g., motion to compel, motion for protective order, motion to dismiss)?
- What discovery should we prioritize immediately (emails, device images, respondents’ financials)?
- When should we schedule depositions (respondent, witnesses, expert witnesses)?
- Are there sanctions or fee-shifting options (e.g., if opposing counsel acted in bad faith)? How likely are fee awards?
- Should we seek to bifurcate custody-related matters if they are only tangentially related?

How I can help next
- I can draft a concise email or checklist for Maria Santos with the questions above.
- I can create a one-page timeline template and an exhibit index you can fill in.
- I can draft a mock Q&A for practicing answers to the most likely lines of questioning.

Tell me:
1) Which type of hearing it is (if you know), and
2) Which of the follow-up items you’d like me to do now (draft email to Maria, mock Q&A, one-page timeline template, exhibit index), and I’ll prepare it.

