I can help you prepare. I’m not your lawyer, so treat this as organizational and preparatory — confirm everything with Maria Santos at Santos Law Office. Below are focused questions to ask your attorney, likely lines of questioning the judge or opposing counsel may use, and practical prep (documents, witnesses, courtroom conduct, and next steps).

1) Quick reminder to ask Maria first
- Confirm what the upcoming “preliminary hearing” is called and its purpose in this case (status conference, TRO/PI hearing, evidentiary prelim, case management, etc.).
- Ask Maria how she plans to use the hearing (scheduling, preserve evidence, get temporary relief, narrow issues, or preview evidence for trial).
- Ask whether there are local San Diego County civil division rules or protocols we must follow (filings, exhibits, chambers procedures).

2) High-priority questions to ask Maria Santos
- What exactly will the judge be focused on at this hearing? (procedural schedule, temporary orders, evidence sufficiency)
- What burden of proof applies to each claim/defense we have (e.g., breach of contract, injunctive relief)?
- What facts are most critical to prove our case and which facts opposing counsel will try to undermine?
- How might the respondent use financial or custody information against me, and how can we limit that?
- What documents and communications do you want me to produce now and later? Are any documents privileged or protected?
- Should we file any emergency motions (TRO, protective order, motion to seal, or in limine)? If so, why and when?
- Are there discovery deadlines, and how should I preserve evidence (emails, chat logs, devices, metadata)?
- Do we need expert witnesses (for damages, forensic analysis) — if so, when do we designate them and what will they say?
- What’s our settlement posture and what settlement authority do you need from me?
- What witnesses will testify for us and for the respondent? Do we need subpoenas?
- Will custody/financial details be part of civil discovery? If yes, how can we protect family/custody proceedings from being used here?
- What are the likely defenses they’ll raise (e.g., ambiguity, lack of consideration, public policy, consent, waiver), and how do we counter them?
- How should I answer questions under oath — any “lines” to avoid or phrasing to use?
- Can we run a mock direct and cross-examination together? When?
- What are likely deadlines and next milestones if the hearing does not resolve the case?

3) Likely questions the judge may ask (so your attorney can prepare answers)
- What is the precise relief you are seeking now (injunction, damages, declaratory relief, fees)?
- What evidence do you have that an NDA existed and that it was breached?
- What is the factual and legal basis for emergency relief (if requested)?
- What prejudice will you suffer if interim relief is not granted?
- Have you preserved the relevant evidence? Have any documents been deleted or devices wiped?
- Are there related proceedings (family court, criminal) that affect this matter?
- What is the requested schedule for discovery, motion practice, and trial?
- Have you tried to resolve this outside court (demand letters, mediation)?

4) Likely questions opposing counsel may ask you (to prepare for cross-exam)
About the NDA and its terms
- Did you sign the NDA? When and where? Who witnessed it?
- Was there consideration (something exchanged) when the NDA was formed?
- Who else was present or had access to the confidential information?
- Can you identify the specific portions of the NDA the respondent is accused of violating?
- Did you or anyone authorized by you disclose the information? To whom and when?

About communications/evidence
- Do these emails/messages reflect intent (ask you to explain context)?
- How were the allegedly confidential files shared (attachments, links, USBs)?
- Did you ever post or publish any of the disputed information publicly (social media, blogs)?

About damages and mitigation
- What harm did you suffer from the alleged breach (financial, reputational)?
- What steps did you take to mitigate damages after you learned of the disclosure?

About credibility and background
- Have you made inconsistent statements about key facts? (They will try to highlight inconsistencies.)
- Have you had disputes with the respondent before (pattern or motive)?
- Questions about finances, employment, assets — if they argue damages or punitive relief relates to your finances.
- Custody/family questions — if opposing counsel tries to attack credibility or suggest motivation; ask Maria how to limit or object to those lines.

5) How custody/financial details might be used and what to ask Maria
- Ask whether those issues are relevant to the NDA claim or whether they’re a fishing expedition.
- Ask how to object to irrelevant or prejudicial inquiries and whether to seek a protective order.
- Ask whether to produce financial documents or provide redacted versions, and whether to use privileged/sensitive-designation or motion to seal.
- If custody matters are separate in family court, ask how to prevent cross-use and whether there are family-law confidentiality protections.

6) Evidence and documents checklist to prepare and ask Maria about
- Original signed NDA(s) and any amendments or side agreements
- All communications about the NDA: emails, texts, DMs, memos, phone logs, calendar entries
- Documents showing what information was designated confidential and when
- Copies of the allegedly disclosed material and logs of when/where it appeared
- Evidence of harm (invoices, lost business, emails showing reputational damage)
- Metadata and forensic copies (if applicable) — ask Maria whether to image devices
- Witness statements and contact info for witnesses
- Any settlement/demand letters, prior correspondence with opposing counsel
- Financial records if damages are at issue (but check with Maria before producing)
- Court filings and local civil rules for San Diego County division

7) Practice answers and courtroom demeanor
- Practice short, direct, truthful answers. Don’t speculate. If you don’t know, say “I don’t know” or “I don’t recall” (but you may refresh recollection with documents under your attorney’s guidance).
- Avoid volunteering extra information or telling a long narrative — stick to the facts.
- Be prepared to authenticate documents you produced: dates, senders, recipients.
- Dress conservatively, arrive early, turn off phones, follow courtroom etiquette.
- Expect to be sworn in; ask Maria how to handle leading questions and improper foundation questions.

8) Requests to make of your attorney now (action items)
- Conduct a mock direct examination and cross-examination with you.
- Prepare an evidence timeline/calendar and a short witness statement for the record.
- Draft and file any necessary emergency or protective motions now if needed.
- Confirm discovery plan and preservation steps, including forensic imaging if warranted.
- Identify and prepare key witnesses; prepare subpoenas if needed.
- Discuss settlement strategy and a target range/terms you would accept.
- Ask Maria to flag any privacy measures (sealed exhibits, redactions, protective orders) and the costs/risks of public filings.

9) Trial-focused preparation (if the case proceeds)
- Discuss standard of proof and elements you must prove at trial.
- Identify expert witness needs (for damages, forensic authentication).
- Plan exhibit lists, witness order, direct/cross outlines.
- Discuss motions in limine to exclude damaging or irrelevant material (including custody/financial matters if irrelevant).
- Discuss jury vs. bench trial strategy (if applicable) — what type of judge/jury questions to expect.

10) Short practice Q&A examples to rehearse (sample answers)
- Q: “Did you sign an NDA?” A: “Yes. I signed it on [date].” (Have document ready.)
- Q: “What confidential information was covered?” A: “The NDA defines it as [brief phrase or refer to section].”
- Q: “When did you first learn of the disclosure?” A: “I first learned on [date], when [describe how you learned].”
- Q: “What harm did you suffer?” A: “I suffered [concise damage], supported by [document X].”
- Q: “Have you discussed settlement?” A: “We discussed settlement in [date], and I gave/received a demand of [amount or term], subject to counsel.” (Don’t volunteer terms without counsel’s OK.)

11) Final checklist to bring to your meeting with Maria
- Copies of all NDA-related documents and communications (organized chronologically)
- A concise written timeline of events (dates, actions, witnesses)
- List of people who know about the situation and their contact info
- Any evidence of damages or mitigation
- Notes about any relevant custody/financial issues and their dates
- A list of concrete questions from this list you most want answers to

If you want, I can:
- Turn the “questions to ask Maria” into a printable one-page checklist.
- Create a mock cross-examination script you can use in a practice session.
- Draft a timeline template you can fill with dates and documents.

