Good — this is exactly the kind of preparation that helps you feel confident and avoids surprises. Below I’ve organized (A) focused questions you should ask your attorney, Maria Santos, (B) likely questions a judge or opposing counsel might ask you (or your lawyer) at the preliminary hearing or in depositions/trial, (C) practical preparation steps and documents to gather, and (D) short tips for answering under oath and working with your lawyer. Use these with Maria to turn them into a concrete plan and practice.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case fundamentals & strategy
  - What specific legal elements the plaintiff/defendant must prove in this NDA dispute under California law?
  - What is our theory of the case and the respondent’s theory? Where are our main strengths and vulnerabilities?
  - What is the immediate purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing (status conference, discovery scheduling, temporary relief, etc.) and what outcome should we aim for there?

- Procedural & timeline
  - What are the key upcoming deadlines (discovery cutoff, motions in limine, exchange of exhibits, trial date)?
  - Will the hearing be in-person or remote, and how long should I expect it to last?
  - What is the expected timeline from preliminary hearing to trial (typical San Diego County timeline for civil division)?

- Discovery, evidence & confidentiality
  - What discovery will the respondent likely request (documents, communications, depositions)? How should we respond?
  - Given the financial and custody information involved, should we seek a protective order or to seal filings? What form should that take?
  - Are there specific documents I must preserve to avoid spoliation claims?

- Witnesses & proof
  - Which witnesses do we plan to call? Which adverse witnesses will they call?
  - What documentary proof do we need to prove breach, damages, or lack of consent/authorization?
  - Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., damages, forensic analyst)?

- Depositions & testimony prep
  - Can we schedule mock depositions and courtroom Q&A practice? How should I answer typical lines of questioning?
  - What documents should I bring to my depositions or hearings?
  - How should I handle questions touching on custody or finances to minimize unnecessary exposure?

- Motions & pretrial requests
  - Should we file any pre-hearing motions (temporary injunction, motion for protective order, motion to dismiss, motion for summary judgment later)?
  - Are there likely motions in limine we should prepare (to exclude certain evidence)?

- Settlement & costs
  - What are realistic settlement ranges and bargaining posture? What confidentiality terms should be demanded?
  - What are estimated fees and costs to trial? Are fee-shifting provisions available under the NDA or statute?

- Communication & logistics
  - How will you prepare me for hearing day (scripts, exhibits labeled, where to stand/sit, courtroom protocol)?
  - How should I communicate with you between now and the hearing? (turnaround time, billing on calls)
  - If opposing counsel asks to meet/mediate, how should I respond?

B. Questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask (prepare short, truthful answers)
- Judge (at preliminary hearing/status conference)
  - Are the parties properly before the court? (service, representation)
  - Is there any pending or needed emergency relief (temporary restraining order, preliminary injunction)?
  - What discovery is outstanding? Do you need changes to schedule or additional time?
  - Are there settlement efforts or willingness to mediate?
  - How long will trial take and how many witnesses/exhibits?
  - Any confidentiality issues or requests to seal pleadings?

- Opposing counsel (in discovery, deposition, or trial)
  - Background and relationship questions: “How do you know the respondent? Describe your working/personal relationship.”
  - Contract formation and terms: “When did you sign the NDA? Where and why? Did you receive consideration? Were any terms changed?”
  - Alleged breach details: “What exact disclosures are you alleging? When did they occur? To whom? What were the means of disclosure (email, phone, in-person)?”
  - Intent & knowledge: “Did you authorize the disclosure? Did you know the information was confidential? Did you take steps to protect it?”
  - Damages & mitigation: “What financial or other harms did you suffer? When did you learn of the disclosure? What steps did you take to mitigate damages?”
  - Custody/financial lines (they may probe if relevant): “How do the custody or financial details relate to the information at issue?” — these can be used to attack credibility or motive.
  - Credibility & consistency: Questions about dates, prior statements, communications that can be used to impeach.
  - Document authenticity: “Is this your email/text? Did you send this message?”

C. Documents and materials to prepare (bring to your attorney meeting and hearing)
- The signed NDA and any drafts, amendments, addendums
- All communications relevant to the NDA: emails, texts, DMs, letters (with metadata/timestamps if possible)
- Chronological timeline of events (dates, times, who said/did what)
- Evidence of disclosure (copies/screenshots of the allegedly disclosed material, witness statements)
- Financial records that show damages or losses you claim (bank statements, invoices, lost-contract evidence)
- Custody orders or documents relevant if custody matters touch the dispute
- Pleadings: complaint, answers, counterclaims, any motions filed
- Discovery responses you’ve served or received
- Contacts for potential witnesses (names, phone/email)
- Any expert reports or retained experts you may need to hire
- Prior settlement offers or communications (do not disclose privileged communications without attorney review)

D. Practical tips for testimony and working with counsel
- Keep answers short and direct; don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” instead of guessing.
- If a question is ambiguous or compound, ask for clarification or for questions to be asked one at a time.
- Avoid arguing with opposing counsel or becoming emotional; stay factual.
- Don’t sign or send anything to opposing counsel without Maria reviewing it first.
- Ask Maria to run mock Q&A sessions (both direct and cross-examination style).
- Ask for a protective order if the other side is likely to request sensitive personal info (financial/custody).
- Preserve all potentially relevant data and devices — don’t delete messages, social posts, etc. Tell Maria immediately about any potential spoliation risk.

E. Specific requests you can make of Maria before the hearing
- Conduct a mock deposition/cross-exam and record it so you can practice answers.
- Prepare a short, bullet-point script of your key facts and chronology you must state consistently.
- Draft a motion for protective order or to seal sensitive exhibits if needed.
- Identify and prepare any witnesses and coordinate subpoenas.
- Prepare an exhibit list and labeling system for the hearing/trial.

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn these into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your meeting with Maria.
- Help draft a concise timeline of events if you give me the key dates and facts.
- Create a short practice Q&A script (sample questions and suggested short answers) for you to rehearse.

Which of those would be most helpful next — the printable checklist, a timeline draft, or a practice Q&A script?

